{"@graph":[{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/","identifier":"content:post-agi-economics-home","@type":"WebPage","name":"When intelligence gets cheap","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"description":"Research into production, scarcity, distribution and the models we use when cognitive work becomes cheaper.","dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Underlying film\nWhen intelligence gets cheap\nWhat remains scarce?\nProduction, ownership and ordinary life.\nA useful answer gets cheaper. A home, a power connection or a trustworthy decision may remain difficult to obtain.\nAs machines take on more cognitive work, what becomes possible, what remains limiting, and who gets to use the gain?\nExplore the research\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"about":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/#entity","identifier":"project:post-agi-economics","@type":"ResearchProject","name":"Post-AGI Economics","subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/","identifier":"content:post-agi-economics-home","@type":"WebPage","name":"When intelligence gets cheap","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/about/","identifier":"content:post-agi-economics-about","@type":"AboutPage","name":"About Post-AGI Economics","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"description":"What Post-AGI Economics studies, the three lines of inquiry it follows, and how One Yardstick, MONAD and RawThoughts connect to it.","text":"When machines can do more of the work, what changes for the people living in the economy?\nPost-AGI Economics studies how cheaper machine agency changes production, scarcity, ownership and income. More capability can open new activities while making power, infrastructure or access more valuable. The gains depend on how firms, households and institutions adapt, and on who can participate.\nWhere the constraint moves\nA task can become feasible before a firm can deploy it. Deployment may expose a shortage of power, infrastructure or another input. Investment can relieve that shortage while creating another source of scarcity.\nFollow the constraints\nWho gets the gain\nA production gain does not tell us who can afford the result. Prices, wages, ownership and public arrangements carry that gain into household resources in different ways.\nFollow the gain\nModels and measures\nSometimes a familiar model needs new values. Sometimes another decision-maker or relationship has entered the activity. More detail earns its place when it restores a connection that changes the answer.\nExamine the models\nA model can be right about its totals and still miss the relation needed for a decision.\nAn economy can produce more while some households lose the income with which they bought its output. Firms can become more productive while a scarce input captures much of the gain. Following those relationships requires a model that represents the actors, constraints and channels relevant to the decision.\nThat question connects the programme to MONAD’s study of changing descriptions: when does a model need new values, and when must it make room for a different account of the activity?\nAsk what changes the answer.\nA workshop can gain many more plans while its machine time still limits production. In that case, the familiar relation can survive while the bottleneck moves. Two purchasing agents can spend the same amount yet select parts that no longer fit together. For that question, the account needs to retain who chose which part.\nThese worked comparisons give the programme a practical test for richer models. Firm and household studies then ask which mechanisms occur outside the construction. Models and measures keeps that observation question beside the economic one.\nHow much time does an economy have to adapt?\nOne Yardstick connects the evaluation of agent execution to a proposed transition clock: how much time might an economy have to adapt, and what kind of economy would it become? That living thesis develops alongside the inquiry into moving constraints and rents. A capability becoming accessible, a firm changing its process and a household losing income are different transitions. Understanding their relationship is part of the work needed to connect a clock to the economy it describes.\nPost-AGI Economics is a research programme within RawThoughts, with this independent publication. Harsh Joshi is its author; RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited is its publisher.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/about/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/#entity","identifier":"project:monad","@type":"CreativeWork","name":"MONAD","subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/","identifier":"content:causality-project-monad","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD","url":"https://causality.in/monad/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/#article","identifier":"content:monad-docs","@type":"Article","name":"Descriptions and repair","url":"https://causality.in/monad/descriptions-and-repair/"},{"@id":"https://causality.in/monad/mentions/","identifier":"content:monad-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"MONAD mentions","url":"https://causality.in/monad/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.causality.in/monad/overview"}]},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/#entity","identifier":"project:one-yardstick","@type":"CreativeWork","name":"One Yardstick","subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/","identifier":"content:post-agi-one-yardstick","@type":"WebPage","name":"One Yardstick","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/mentions/","identifier":"content:one-yardstick-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"One Yardstick mentions","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/mentions/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/","identifier":"content:post-agi-economics-articles","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Blog","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":5,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/cheap-copies-scarce-attention/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/cheap-copies-scarce-attention/","identifier":"content:post-agi-attention","name":"Cheap copies, scarce attention"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/history-without-a-timetable/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/history-without-a-timetable/","identifier":"content:post-agi-history-mechanisms","name":"A catalogue still needs an audience"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-clock-many-frontiers/#article","@type":"TechArticle","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-clock-many-frontiers/","identifier":"content:post-agi-clock-limits","name":"The time between frontiers"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-yardstick-one-clock/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-yardstick-one-clock/","identifier":"content:post-agi-one-yardstick-one-clock","name":"One Yardstick, One Clock: why the AI timeline debate misses the point"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":5,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/the-red-pill/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/the-red-pill/","identifier":"content:post-agi-the-red-pill","name":"The Red Pill: AI skews the power law. And that's okay!"}}],"itemListOrder":"https://schema.org/ItemListOrderDescending"},"description":"Independent essays on exchanging intelligence, comparing human and agent work, timing a transition, information markets and media history.","text":"","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/cheap-copies-scarce-attention/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-attention","@type":"Article","name":"Cheap copies, scarce attention","headline":"Cheap copies, scarce attention","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["information markets","attention scarcity","cost of producing information","verification","niche audiences","recommendation and filtering"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/cheap-copies-scarce-attention/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Cheap copies, scarce attention","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/cheap-copies-scarce-attention/"},"description":"Cheaper information changes selection, specialist markets and the feedback between audience access and product improvement.","datePublished":{"@value":"2026-11-18","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Cheap copies, scarce attention\nMaking information becomes a different economic activity when producing another version is cheap. A specialist explanation can reach a smaller audience, a creator can explore more alternatives and a useful service can be adapted to a need that once cost too much to serve. The cost reduction opens possibilities while changing where the difficult work lies. Attention, verification and distribution still shape which possibilities become valuable. How do these complements change as production and selection become easier, and who receives the gain?\nThe Red Pill asks whether reusable intelligence could itself become a unit of exchange. This article follows a different part of that economic problem: how easier production changes selection, distribution and the value of reaching a particular audience.\nWhen making gets cheap, choosing gets harder\nImagine a specialist reader who once chose among ten useful reports and now faces a thousand. The reader's time is unchanged, so inspecting every report is no longer feasible. Search, recommendations, reputation and trusted relationships become part of finding something worth reading. The producer and the selector need not be the same actor. A lower production cost can increase the value of selection, or better filtering can reduce the cost of finding a useful source. The outcome depends on the reader's purpose. Entertainment, a consequential decision and learning a difficult subject place different values on variety, error and depth. A system optimising engagement may perform a different selection from one helping someone make a decision.\nThe work inside the knowledge good\nFluent text is one part of a useful report. Interviews, experiments, source access, verification and judgement can remain expensive. If checking dominates the cost, faster drafting may produce only a modest saving. If a tool helps compare evidence or locate inconsistencies, its contribution can be larger because it changes another stage of the work.\nSuppose, only for illustration, that preparing a specialist report takes one hundred units of active work: forty for research, twenty for drafting and forty for checking. Reducing drafting to two units lowers the whole requirement to eighty-two, not ten. If the same tool also halves the work of finding relevant sources without degrading the result, another twenty units become available. The economic contribution depends on the stages it changes.\nStage\tEarlier requirement\tFaster drafting\tFaster drafting and source finding\t\nResearch\t40\t40\t20\t\nDrafting\t20\t2\t2\t\nChecking\t40\t40\t40\t\nTotal active work\t100\t82\t62\t\nThese are assumed workloads, not product results. They make verification's role explicit rather than placing it inside a vague “human in the loop” surcharge.\nCreative production has a similar division. Generating alternatives can make exploration less costly; selection, coherence, rights and a distinctive practice give the result its particular value. A useful account follows that whole process instead of treating the count of generated outputs as completed work.\nAn explanation and a reusable capability are also different goods. A working support procedure, for example, may carry knowledge of a task, the conditions under which it succeeds and the means of checking its result. Its useful transfer depends on those conditions being met in the receiving setting. Reusing that capability therefore includes adapting its dependencies and checking that its result still means what the recipient needs. Economically, reuse could reduce repeated setup while creating work around adaptation, verification and responsibility. That is a more specific exchange than selling another copy of a description.\nA smaller audience can become worth serving\nLower creation cost can make a specialised report or service viable at a smaller scale. This is a real change in the feasible business model: a need too narrow to support a costly production process may sustain a cheaper one. Its income still depends on demand and access. A reader can want the specialised work and lack money or time to buy it. Several producers can discover the same need, competing much of the saving into lower prices. A platform can control access to the audience, while a direct relationship may give the producer another route. Willingness and ability to pay, alternatives, distribution cost and retention determine which of these arrangements lasts. Relevance therefore creates a reason to choose, not a guaranteed margin. Lower cost can benefit the audience even when competition leaves little surplus with any one producer.\nDistribution can feed back into the product\nImagine two services making equally useful reports. One already reaches many readers; the other has to find each reader from scratch. If the first can observe which questions recur, which explanations resolve them and why readers leave, its distribution can support faster improvement. Better fit can then bring more readers and more observations. Cheap production amplifies that loop when the service can afford to try more versions between observations.\nThe loop is conditional. Clicks may reward a provocative title rather than a sound explanation. A large general audience may teach less about a specialist need than a small, direct relationship with the people who have it. More observations also help only if the service can interpret and use them; permitted access to feedback is a different asset from merely serving a large readership. Distribution is an advantage here through the learning it supports, not because size automatically supplies a better product.\nThat makes a narrow audience interesting in another way. A producer who understands a recurring, costly question can improve around it without first winning a mass attention market. Lower production cost can make that relationship economical. The competing possibilities are a growing incumbent's feedback advantage and a specialist's closer fit, with selection costs and the audience's purposes deciding how either becomes useful.\nDelegating the selection\nSummarising, filtering and retrieval move part of the selection process into systems. A person might then inspect fewer sources directly while using more of their content. The constraint changes again: source quality, permitted access, objectives and the ability to inspect a consequential conclusion become part of the service.\nA producer may optimise for the filter rather than the ultimate reader. What the filter lets through influences whose work becomes visible, just as a publication's editorial policy or a search ranking can. Processing a document also differs from granting it authority: an agent needs a reason to rely on a claim, not merely the ability to extract its sentences.\nThe economic question follows what useful selection costs, whose purposes it serves and what control the user retains. Delegation can make a wider range of material usable without eliminating the relationships and responsibilities around its use.\nWhere the saving goes\nCheaper production can widen participation, intensify competition or make an intermediary more important. Effective filters could reduce selection costs; direct relationships could weaken intermediary dependence; better source quality could make the larger supply more useful. These alternatives belong inside the argument about the market, rather than after an assumed concentration outcome. Ownership and competition determine who retains a return from the changing constraints. The measure of useful output determines which part of the gain becomes visible. The finished good is something somebody can use, trust or enjoy. Its economics lies in the work and relationships that make that possible.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/cheap-copies-scarce-attention/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/history-without-a-timetable/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-history-mechanisms","@type":"Article","name":"A catalogue still needs an audience","headline":"A catalogue still needs an audience","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["technological transitions","broadcast schedules","on-demand catalogues","attention metrics","streaming royalties","conversational interfaces"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/history-without-a-timetable/","@type":"WebPage","name":"A catalogue still needs an audience","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/history-without-a-timetable/"},"description":"Broadcasting, streaming and conversational interfaces reveal how cheaper delivery changes selection, income and the work behind a service.","datePublished":{"@value":"2026-11-17","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"A catalogue still needs an audience\nWhen I was young, our family sat together on Saturday evenings to watch Jai Hanuman on Doordarshan, and woke up to Chitrahar on Sundays. When the picture became noisy, one of us (it was always me) went upstairs, climbed onto the roof and adjusted the antenna. A Yagi–Uda antenna, for the electronics geek. No remote, one channel, knobs and a twisted piece of metal on the roof.\nBy the time I wrote Evolution of AI, and its future in B2B in May 2024, each family member could watch the show they wanted, when they wanted, on a device in their pocket. They could also become a creator. The important change was how people lived around the medium: a shared schedule became individual choices, with new expectations and new ways of reaching an audience.\nA transmission slot gives a programme a time and an audience somewhere to gather. An on-demand catalogue loosens that constraint. Yet making the next copy available does not make somebody watch it. The scarce thing has moved. The B2B essay follows that change into the organisation behind a conversational front desk; Radio became Spotify follows the need, habits and institutional memory through the wider transition. Here the economic question is what happens to selection, payment and the work behind easier access.\nFrom a schedule to a selection problem\nA broadcast schedule coordinates many viewers around one sequence. An on-demand catalogue permits different sequences, leaving each viewer with the work of finding and choosing. Producing another copy becomes easier; reaching a particular person remains a separate problem. Recommendation, editorial selection, search and the relationship between creator and audience acquire roles that differ from a transmission slot.\nThe measures follow that change. YouTube distinguishes thumbnail impressions, views, estimated unique viewers and watch time. A programme can be widely exposed and scarcely watched, or watched deeply by a smaller audience. These observations describe different aspects of attention. When rewards depend on one of them, people may change how they package and produce their work to meet it. What becomes easy to distribute and what becomes profitable to make can diverge.\nRights travel through the new distribution system\nStreaming makes a catalogue accessible through a service, while recordings, compositions and contractual claims remain with their rightsholders. Spotify describes payments to recording and publishing rightsholders, with artists and songwriters paid according to their agreements, rather than through a universal fixed per-stream rate. The cost of delivering a copy and the contractual path of its income are therefore separate parts of the arrangement. Cheaper delivery can coexist with concentrated catalogue rights or a new intermediary. The constraint may move toward access to the work, access to the audience, or the ability to retain a share of payment.\nUploading changes who can participate. Earning a livelihood also requires sustained demand and a claim on the resulting income. As copies become cheaper, selection and distribution still shape which work reaches an audience and through which relationship its income returns.\nThe front desk and the organisation behind it\nA conversational front desk raises a related economic possibility. Instead of translating a need into a company's menus, a person states the need and the organisation works out how to meet it. The simpler surface asks more of the business: records must be available, responsibility clear and a request connected to a means of fulfilment. Information, exception handling and commercial relationships become part of the interface's practical reach. The valuable change is the reorganisation that makes the simpler surface useful.\nThose complements can support businesses in their own right. An integration service may be valuable while systems remain incompatible. A common standard could remove one part of its function while changing relationships create another. Its duration depends on the work it performs and the alternatives that become available.\nThe paths that survive in memory\nThe move from a family television to separate screens makes a change in everyday organisation visible. Household income, networks, rights, devices, preferences and business models changed alongside distribution. To carry a mechanism from that history into another setting, we need to ask which conditions travel with it. A fixed and known audience may face little discovery cost even when production becomes cheap. Different rights can distribute the same revenue differently. Existing infrastructure can permit a faster deployment than a system that first needs years of construction; organisational learning or liability can still slow it down. Failed products and persistent older forms belong in the comparison because they reveal the alternatives a successful path can hide.\nFor cheaper cognition, the question returns in a new form. Once producing an answer becomes easier, finding the relevant answer, trusting it and acting on it can become more valuable. The firm that supplies those complements may capture income, face new competition or make its own service unnecessary. A catalogue needs an audience; an answer needs a place in someone's work. Following that relationship gives the history something concrete to contribute.\nYouTube's account of content metrics, checked 16 November 2026. Definitions and denominators differ by metric and format; they are not interchangeable counts of people or useful attention.\nSpotify's royalty guide, checked 16 November 2026, describes the service's payment arrangements. It supports the distinction between delivery, the right to receive payment and the artist's contractual share; it does not establish every streaming market's distribution of income.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/history-without-a-timetable/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-clock-many-frontiers/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-clock-limits","@type":"TechArticle","name":"The time between frontiers","headline":"The time between frontiers","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["absorbing Markov chains","time-varying transitions","waiting times","Weibull survival","AI adoption frontiers"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-clock-many-frontiers/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The time between frontiers","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-clock-many-frontiers/","relatedLink":["https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/a-forecast-meets-its-outcome/","https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/measuring-ai-diffusion/","https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-yardstick-one-clock/"]},"description":"The same present crossing probability can imply different waiting times when future transition rules differ.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-09-15","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The time between frontiers\nTwo frontiers each have a ten percent chance of being crossed in the next period. One keeps that chance in every later period; the other rises to fifty percent after the first. Their present probabilities agree. Their expected waiting times do not.\nThat small distinction carries a large question about AI's economic transition. A model of a task can improve, a firm can gain access, a deployment can become reliable and an institution can permit a new arrangement. Each movement can change the conditions for another. A clock needs to follow those processes closely enough to make the waiting meaningful. Its apparent precision is worth little if the rule governing tomorrow's transitions remains hidden.\nNote\nAssumed transition laws. The constructions below use stipulated transition laws, not fitted adoption data. They isolate what a waiting-time calculation depends on before asking which process observations would support.\nThe future transition rule matters\nFor a finite, fixed absorbing Markov chain, the transient matrix Q describes movement among states before the terminal event. If its spectral radius is less than one, the fundamental matrix and expected steps to absorption are:\n\nN=(I-Q)^{-1},\\qquad \\boldsymbol{t}=N\\boldsymbol{1}.\n\nHere \\boldsymbol{1} is a column of ones; the row sums of N count expected periods before the crossing. Grinstead and Snell's probability text develops the fixed-process result.\nConsider a frontier with a 10 percent chance of being crossed in each period, independently while it remains uncrossed. Its expected waiting time is ten periods. Now let the probability be 10 percent in the first period and 50 percent in every later period. One period passes in all cases. With probability 0.9 the frontier remains uncrossed, and the remaining expectation is two periods:\n\n\\mathbb{E}[T]=1+0.9\\times2=2.8\\text{ periods}.\n\nRaising later crossing probabilities from 0.1 to 0.5 shortens the survival tail and expected wait from ten to 2.8 periods, despite the shared first-period probability of 0.1.\nThe top panel shows the probability of still waiting after each period; the lower panel shows the probability of the first crossing in that period. The curves continue beyond the displayed sixteen periods; each mean summarises the whole waiting-time distribution.\nThe two paths have the same current crossing probability and different waiting times. Today's probability becomes a countdown only after we specify how the rule behaves in the future.\nWith changing transient matrices and initial row distribution \\alpha, survival after k periods is:\n\n\\Pr(T>k)=\\alpha Q_0Q_1\\cdots Q_{k-1}\\boldsymbol{1}.\n\nAt k=0 the product is the identity and survival is one. Summing these survival probabilities gives the expected waiting time when the sum is finite. Freezing the current matrix answers a different conditional question: how long would the transition take if today's rule persisted?\nA clock with two transient states\nTake a process with states “awaiting setup”, “ready” and “crossed”. In each period, the awaiting state stays put with probability one half, reaches readiness with probability one quarter and crosses directly with probability one quarter. The ready state either stays or crosses, each with probability one half. Once crossed, the process stays crossed:\n\nP=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n0.5&0.25&0.25\\\\\n0&0.5&0.5\\\\\n0&0&1\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nQ=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n0.5&0.25\\\\\n0&0.5\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\nConsequently:\n\nN=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n2&1\\\\\n0&2\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\boldsymbol{t}=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n3\\\\\n2\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\nStarting before setup gives an expectation of three periods; starting ready gives two. The difference comes from the transition law, not from the names assigned to the states.\nStates, transitions and uncertainty\nEach row of a transition matrix is a probability distribution: its entries are non-negative and sum to one. Independently fitted logistic curves need not satisfy that joint condition. If their probabilities sum above one, assigning a remainder to the final state gives a negative probability. The transition construction has to preserve the row before its uncertainty is sampled.\nThe starting distribution also needs a meaning. A filtered distribution expresses what observations imply about the current state. A scenario distribution specifies an assumed starting point. A quasi-stationary distribution describes the state conditional on not yet being absorbed. The transient block loses probability through absorption; an ordinary stationary probability distribution is not interchangeable with these choices. Uncertainty bands inherit those choices. They can describe uncertainty in fitted parameters under a specified process without covering a different process, a changed observation rule or a new institutional arrangement.\nElapsed time is part of the state\nIn a fixed absorbing chain with initial row vector \\alpha, the probability of remaining transient after k steps is \\alpha Q^k\\boldsymbol{1}. The waiting time has a discrete phase-type distribution. A negative-binomial approximation would need its own conditions and an account of approximation error; adding more states does not supply either.\nSome crossings depend on the time already spent waiting. A project awaiting permission for a day may face different prospects from one awaiting it for a year. For a Weibull survival function S(u)=\\exp(-\\lambda u^\\kappa), with \\lambda,\\kappa>0, the probability of surviving a further t after already surviving s is:\n\n\\Pr(T>s+t\\mid T>s)\n=\\frac{S(s+t)}{S(s)}\n=\\exp\\left[-\\lambda\\big((s+t)^\\kappa-s^\\kappa\\big)\\right].\n\nElapsed duration affects conditional survival; when \\kappa=1, this expression reduces to the memoryless exponential case. A clock whose state omits relevant duration can confuse these different waiting situations.\nWhat crossing the frontier means\nAbsorption is a strong mathematical condition: the process cannot leave its terminal state. Economic persistence can be weaker. A technology may become entrenched through integration and switching costs while remaining vulnerable to restrictions, changed prices or a better alternative. One firm's lock-in need not be another firm's lack of choice.\nThe measured event also matters. A share of firms using assistants describes something different from a share of valuable work executed autonomously. A benchmark score describes capability under a test; deployed coverage includes operating conditions. A revised survey question can move the reported adoption series without changing any workflow on that date. These definitions determine the frontier the clock would time.\nLocal clocks in a changing economy\nThe economy-wide clock in One Yardstick proposes following regimes towards agent-dominant execution, with different destinations according to whether households retain purchasing power. That ambition remains part of the inquiry. A local clock asks which task or sector frontier can be timed, through which transition process, and with which observations locating the system within it. The relationship between these scales has to be established rather than assumed.\nSuch a model could compare conditional signal paths, describe heterogeneous diffusion or estimate a waiting time under a stated process. Its forecast would then have to meet observed outcomes under a fixed event definition and information cutoff. Crossing one frontier can change another frontier's conditions. The useful question is which crossing we can describe, what moves it, and what work remains on the other side. The economy does not need to keep one appointment for those local questions to matter.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-clock-many-frontiers/survival-and-waiting.svg","description":"Raising later crossing probabilities from 0.1 to 0.5 shortens the survival tail and expected wait from ten to 2.8 periods, despite the shared first-period probability of 0.1.","caption":"The top panel shows the probability of still waiting after each period; the lower panel shows the probability of the first crossing in that period. The curves continue beyond the displayed sixteen periods; each mean summarises the whole waiting-time distribution.\n"}],"url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-clock-many-frontiers/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-yardstick-one-clock/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-one-yardstick-one-clock","@type":"Article","name":"One Yardstick, One Clock: why the AI timeline debate misses the point","headline":"One Yardstick, One Clock: why the AI timeline debate misses the point","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["AI timelines","Agent Execution Hypothesis","value functions and evaluators","workflow instrumentation","transition clocks","household purchasing power","human and agent execution"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-yardstick-one-clock/","@type":"WebPage","name":"One Yardstick, One Clock: why the AI timeline debate misses the point","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-yardstick-one-clock/","relatedLink":["https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/productivity-and-purchasing-power/","https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-clock-many-frontiers/","https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/evaluation-and-execution/","https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/a-forecast-meets-its-outcome/"]},"description":"Compare human and agent execution on the same work, then ask how much time an economy has to adapt and who can buy the result.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-08-31T12:27:11.670Z","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"One Yardstick, One Clock: why the AI timeline debate misses the point\n“AI will take your job.”\nCool. But AI won't buy Nike, pay for Netflix or need a Tesla in the garage. If you replace workers faster than you create buyers, who exactly are you selling to?\nMost takes stop there: doom or denial. I'm bored of both. It's time we put the pundits to a test. What I'm proposing is simpler: fix the yardstick and read the clock.\nThe yardstick judges execution against a measurable value function, a valid evaluator for that value and actual means to act: permissions, APIs and tools. Same inputs, same tools, same constraints. The clock asks how the surrounding economy changes, how long people have to adapt and which destination that change makes possible.\nNote\nTime to Agent-Dominant Execution is research, with calculations to test and observations still to fit. It has not produced an empirical forecast. A formula for a clock is a beginning; making it tell us something useful about the world is the work.\nThe annoying part nobody wants to hear\nMy starting claim is that many apparent intelligence failures are failures to instrument the work. The value is vague, nobody can reliably judge the result, or the system has no means to act on its answer. A more capable model can still fail in that arrangement. The July 2025 Project NANDA report gave this problem a memorable headline about unsuccessful enterprise AI, but its findings about integration and learning do not establish a universal failure rate or prove my execution hypothesis.\nTake the invoice comparison used below. An assistant can produce a polished payment proposal while missing a discrepancy between the invoice and the authorised order. If the evaluator counts “proposal produced” as “case correctly resolved”, it rewards the appearance of completion. A warranted escalation and a correct proposal need to remain distinguishable from an unauthorised payment. Repairing that measurement makes the capability question answerable; it does not decide the question in the model's favour.\nThis is why I keep saying buy before you build for most teams. The tail from 90 to 99 percent is brutal: edge cases, pipelines, permissions, recovery and reliable operation. Those percentages describe the shape of the effort, not a measured success rate. You win by specifying the value, evaluator and means of action clearly, not by collecting model-zoo stickers. Ship the evaluators with the system.\nOne yardstick to break every tired argument\nPeople argue past each other because they mix governance, what should we optimise, with execution, who can hit that target. Prices already help coordinate people who do not share all their information. Hayek's account of the price system makes that dispersed knowledge the problem to solve. My extension is that a signal need not require a human recipient: a person or a machine can respond if it can interpret the signal and has the tools and permission to act.\nThat leaves the Agent Execution Hypothesis:\nIf a workflow has a measurable value function, a valid evaluator for that value and actual actuators or permissions, an artificial agent can, in principle, match or exceed human execution under the same inputs, tools and constraints.\nThis is a conjecture. Its prerequisites make the comparison possible; they do not prove the conclusion. A working test must name the agent class, human comparison, cases, resources and time available. If the evaluator cannot distinguish a useful result from a bad one, the attempt has not established an executable comparison. If the evaluator works and a candidate falls short, that is a result about the candidate tested. Neither outcome can be dismissed by quietly changing the task.\nThe hypothesis does not wave away tacit skill. If the know-how is in the wrists, its contribution has to become observable in the evaluation. Preferences or inverse reinforcement learning may help in a particular case, but they need validation too. If a valid evaluator cannot be built within the stated limits, the hypothesis does not apply to that attempt yet. Calling a skill unmeasurable and calling it absent are very different things.\nAn invoice comparison puts the distinction into ordinary work. Preparing a correct proposal and escalating an unresolved discrepancy can both be useful outcomes. Releasing money is another action with its own authority. The complete comparison counts review and correction, not just the speed of the first answer.\nThe clock, with its assumptions in view\nThe clock models an economy moving among regimes. Capability, power, labour, credit and governance can change the probability of the next transition. Two proposed destinations distinguish agent-dominant execution with households able to buy from agent-dominant execution with weak household demand. Calling them absorbing states means the model cannot leave them once reached. Whether that is a defensible account of an economy is itself a research question.\nStart with the simpler, fixed-transition calculation. Let Q contain the probabilities of moving among the transient states. When its spectral radius is less than one, the fundamental matrix and expected steps to absorption are\n\nN=(I-Q)^{-1},\\qquad \\boldsymbol t=N\\boldsymbol 1.\n\nFor each initial transient state, the variance of the waiting time is\n\n\\boldsymbol v=(2N-I)\\boldsymbol t-\\boldsymbol t\\odot\\boldsymbol t,\n\nwhere \\odot squares the entries individually. These are properties of the specified chain. They do not supply its probabilities or show that its states describe economic activity.\nChanging probabilities require another calculation. With initial row distribution \\alpha and successive transient matrices Q_0,Q_1,\\ldots, the probability of still waiting after k periods is\n\n\\Pr(T>k)=\\alpha Q_0Q_1\\cdots Q_{k-1}\\boldsymbol 1.\n\nThe order matters. Freezing today's transition matrix answers “what if today's rule persisted?”, not “what happens under tomorrow's changing conditions?”. The time between frontiers works through the difference with two clocks that have the same chance of crossing today and very different expected waits.\nIf the regimes are unobserved, a hidden-state model could estimate them from signals rather than assigning confident labels by hand. That introduces fitting, state-selection and identification questions before a forecast can be trusted.\nAnd a forecast has to meet its outcome. Issue it with the information available at a stated date, keep its original target and compare it with a credible baseline. Proper scoring rules such as Brier and logarithmic scores make confidence consequential. If the forecast underperforms, record the failure and revise under a new plan; don't rewrite the old forecast. A forecast meets its outcome shows how that comparison works.\nThe pre-analysis plan belongs before that outcome: lock the signals, target, fitting choices, baselines and rules for revision so the answer cannot choose its own test afterwards. A Diebold–Mariano comparison can test the average loss difference between two forecast series under its assumptions, with dependence and sample size handled explicitly. It is a comparison of forecasts, not an automatic verdict that one economic theory is true.\nThere is also a reflexivity problem. A published clock can change the investment, policy or behaviour it tries to forecast. The protocol therefore includes disclosure and update rules, an embargo on parameter changes, and monitoring of how the forecast is used.\n“But innovation! But reasoning! But AGI!”\nMachine novelty is possible without settling the question of sentience. AlphaGo's unfamiliar strategies gave human Go players something new to study. Search can surprise us. Whether another system can make a useful novel contribution in another domain remains something to test, not a reason to exclude it by definition.\n“AI needs prompts” also confuses an interface with a system. A programme can respond to a feed, webhook or market signal without somebody typing into a chat window. The useful question is what it can observe, how its response is judged and what it can actually do. A familiar textbox tells us little about that whole arrangement.\nAutomated research could accelerate the change. The possibility of AI systems helping build more capable AI is central to Aschenbrenner's Situational Awareness argument. It belongs here as a possible influence on the transition, not a guaranteed timetable. A clock would need to measure the contribution rather than borrow the confidence of the story.\nAgents still don't buy jeans\nAn agent can perform a job without needing the wages with which its previous worker bought a service. That is why the clock keeps Buyer-Rich and Buyer-Thin outcomes separate. We need to ask what economy the agents are winning into.\nThe buyer question is not answered by counting jobs alone. Lower prices, new work, ownership income, public provision and transfers can change a household's resources. They have different recipients and different conditions. A cheaper service can help while an income loss elsewhere leaves its customer worse off. Following household purchasing power requires those channels to remain visible.\nGovernance decides the objective and the arrangements around the surplus. The clock asks how much time there may be to adapt them. It cannot establish a universal closing window by declaration. That needs a defined intervention, its lead time, a constraint that really becomes binding and evidence about when it does.\nThe fuller Post-AGI Economics inquiry develops alongside this clock. As cognition becomes cheaper, which other inputs become scarce? Who owns them? When does a familiar economic model still work, and when has it omitted the relation needed to understand the result? These questions refine the thesis without making its concern with time disappear.\nIf you build, build like this\nMy engineering scars are why I keep coming back to evaluations as acceptance tests. Start with the value, the evaluator and the permitted actions. Check whether an existing capability can do the job before deciding to build one. Assume integration is the real boss level: the good answer still needs the right data, a dependable handoff and a way to recover when something fails.\nThe yardstick also needs a way to lose. Evaluator failure under the declared budget challenges whether the workflow can be instrumented. A named agent's shortfall challenges that execution claim. Persistent human–agent complementarity challenges a simple replacement story. An outcome that improves the metric while missing its purpose challenges the evaluator. Forecast underperformance challenges the clock. These are different failures, and they point to different work.\nIf you think the yardstick is wrong, propose a better value, evaluator and action specification. If you think the clock is wrong, compare its dated forecast with a better one when there is a forecast to test. Either way, we learn something more useful than another argument about a date.\nFor the capability question, Beyond the Mirage proposes separate measures of causal understanding and agency. Bubble or bridge? takes the execution and complementarity questions into India's AI moment. The Red Pill asks whether transferable intelligence could become a new unit of exchange; Cheap copies, scarce attention follows the constraints around easier production. These are connected questions; a good answer to one does not remove the need to test the others.\nPick a yardstick you can measure. Wire an evaluator you can trust. Give it actuators that actually do things. Then follow the result far enough to find out who gets paid, who gets to buy and how much time they have to adjust. The jeans still need a customer.\nChallapally, Pease, Raskar and Chari, The GenAI Divide: State of AI in Business 2025, July 2025, preliminary findings from January–June 2025. The report combines a review of more than 300 public initiatives, interviews with 52 organisations and 153 conference survey responses. Its definitions and denominators vary; it notes that category sizes and organisational success definitions differ. This supports examining integration and contextual learning, not treating “95 percent” as a representative estimate of all AI projects or a controlled test separating instrumentation from model capability.\nF. A. Hayek, “The Use of Knowledge in Society”, American Economic Review 35(4), September 1945, pp. 519–530. The extension from people responding to price signals to artificial executors is the argument here, not a claim that Hayek established the Agent Execution Hypothesis.\nJames D. Hamilton, “A New Approach to the Economic Analysis of Nonstationary Time Series and the Business Cycle”, Econometrica 57(2), March 1989. Hidden regimes are a modelling option; naming one supplies neither an observed regime nor a fitted transition clock.\nDiebold and Mariano, Comparing Predictive Accuracy, 1994 working paper, published 1995; Diebold's later account of the test's use and limits distinguishes forecast comparison from model selection.\nDemis Hassabis and David Silver, “AlphaGo's next move”, 27 May 2017, describes the strategies and subsequent human experimentation. That example concerns Go, not general economic replacement.\nLeopold Aschenbrenner, “From AGI to Superintelligence: the Intelligence Explosion”, June 2024. This is an acceleration thesis whose conditions and timing need examination, not an empirical result of One Yardstick.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-yardstick-one-clock/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"about":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/#entity","identifier":"project:one-yardstick","@type":"CreativeWork","name":"One Yardstick","subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/","identifier":"content:post-agi-one-yardstick","@type":"WebPage","name":"One Yardstick","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/mentions/","identifier":"content:one-yardstick-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"One Yardstick mentions","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/mentions/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/the-red-pill/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-the-red-pill","@type":"Article","name":"The Red Pill: AI skews the power law. And that's okay!","headline":"The Red Pill: AI skews the power law. And that's okay!","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["power law","intelligence exchange","reusable capabilities","AI quality assurance","attention economics","job displacement","decentralised autonomous organisations"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/the-red-pill/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The Red Pill: AI skews the power law. And that's okay!","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/the-red-pill/"},"description":"A December 2023 thought experiment about exchanging reusable intelligence, the humans who check it and an economy beyond the fight for attention.","datePublished":{"@value":"2023-12-28","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The Red Pill: AI skews the power law. And that's okay!\nJobs are not a law of nature. Humans made jobs as a framework for exchanging value. Money pays for the work; increasingly, attention determines who gets the opportunity to offer it. But neither arrangement exhausts what we could exchange.\nThe Blue Pill scenario starts with a familiar loop. If you already have distribution, you get more deals, more observations and more chances to improve. AI makes the work cheaper and lets you run more experiments through that distribution. The power law was there before AI; AI accelerates it. For the people who put in the work and built their position before this asymmetry arrived, that can be a very good deal. They did build the stage.\nFor someone starting with little or no distribution, it sounds almost like a doomsday scenario. Right?\nWell, welcome to the Matrix, Neo. It's time you realised there is no spoon.\nThe Red Pill scenario asks about a different economy: one where the thing we exchange is intelligence itself. How would we unlock it, and what could somebody without an enormous audience contribute? First, some context is overdue.\nNote\nThis December 2023 thought experiment describes a possible future for transferable intelligence and the economy built around it, not a working market.\nStill early, still closer than it appears\nMy starting assumption is that AI is very early in its development: closer to the vacuum-tube era than the mature transistor era, to borrow an analogy. Even if technical progress accelerates, the change in a business arrives through operations, costs and adoption. The Blue Pill pressure takes time to work through those arrangements.\nGradually is the important word. It is also a dangerous comfort. Humans are not especially good at imagining a change that keeps accelerating. Objects in the mirror are closer than they appear.\nIn The ineffable geometry, I ask whether a richer language for causality could let us understand and localise intelligence inside a model. My ambition there is determinism: an account of why the result follows, beyond the statistical tools on which we currently rely. The continuation explores what such an unfamiliar description might demand of us. These are early mathematical hunches, not established methods for extracting a skill from a neural network.\nThe part I want to carry into this argument is the possibility of transferring intelligence in pieces. Suppose we could identify a useful capability, extract it, preserve what makes it work and combine it with another. If that premise feels too abstract, read the geometry first and come back. It's in cyberspace now; the article is not going anywhere.\nFor this thought experiment, let's roll with it.\nDisplaced into what?\nThe big employment numbers make the stakes obvious and the answer no clearer. McKinsey's 2017 scenarios put 400–800 million people potentially displaced by automation and needing new jobs by 2030. Goldman Sachs's 2023 estimate concerned work equivalent to 300 million full-time jobs exposed to generative-AI automation. Those are different quantities, technologies and scenarios; neither number means that many people have already lost their jobs.\nI am sceptical of the confidence with which such numbers travel. We can paint a number on the problem while disagreeing about what we are measuring, and a hundred things can change between a projection and its outcome. Even after specifying the measure properly, the question remains: displaced into what?\nIn the Red Pill scenario, the human hours required to deliver an outcome become very small. Humans then take a different role: quality assurance and alignment of the intelligence producing it. That is the possibility I want to explore through two deliberately ordinary businesses.\nFirst, sell an apple\nYou sell a product, say an apple, to an Indian bachelor. Everything is fine until you are selling a shit ton of apples to those customers. Now you have support staff, operations and a process that is partly manual and partly technology.\nThree problems start to dominate. Support staff join and leave, taking experience with them. Different people give inconsistent answers. And support needs to coordinate with the supply chain without gaining unrestricted access to sensitive customer or company information.\nMy bet is that AI becomes the cheaper, faster and more consistent way to do this once its actual performance and marginal cost fit the business's timelines and support requirements. That comparison has to include the whole job. A quick answer that creates a refund problem or exposes information has not solved the support problem.\nWho ensures the system gives the output you actually need? It is not a deterministic machine that can simply promise the right answer. During the transition, the support staff perform quality assurance on the interaction between AI and customer. They identify the exception, correct the response and help contain operational knowledge in something the next interaction can use.\nThe apple-support system for Indian bachelors gradually moves from human support towards an AI autopilot. In this scenario, the accumulating checks make the operation more consistent, reduce the disruption caused by staff turnover and preserve the boundaries around information. It becomes the obvious choice when the complete comparison works in its favour.\nThink of the pursuit of a 99.99999% accuracy service-level agreement. Each extra nine can demand disproportionate work; “doubling the effort for the next nine” is my shorthand for that difficult tail, not a measured scaling law or an achieved guarantee. That work is the transition. Calling the system AI does not make the transition disappear.\nThen, sell a banana\nYour colleague sells a banana to the same kind of customer. Why should they go through all that pain again?\nYou should be paid for the effort that made apple support work. Your colleague should be able to reach the market faster. In the Red Pill scenario, they borrow the intelligence for “sell X to an Indian bachelor” and work on the banana-specific part.\nThis assumes we can separate what transfers from what does not. A banana has different properties, complaints and supply arrangements. The transferable capability has to retain its conditions, limits and checks; copying the apple answers would miss the point. The useful unit is the ability to do part of the job, with enough of its surrounding knowledge to make it work elsewhere.\n“I know how to sell a banana.” Neo's next download, perhaps.\nThe Lego intelligence economy, if you will. Extend those two scenarios and start building your Lego land.\nSome things never change follows the practical problem inside that premise: what crosses from one setting to another, what must be adapted and how the recipient checks the result. A reusable description is a beginning; a capability that works in the receiving situation is the proposed good.\nA different unit of exchange\nExtend the framework far enough and I can imagine decentralised autonomous organisations forming around these pieces. A worker no longer has to work only for a particular company. They might maintain and check one particular intelligence Lego, used by many organisations.\nThe people with the most relevant skills perform quality assurance and alignment for that piece. A market values its usefulness; use of one capability creates demand for the complementary capabilities around it. A group of people could earn from making a narrowly defined piece of intelligence reliable across the places that need it.\nThis is why the token matters. When we describe distribution's skewed power law, we are looking at attention on the buying side: who can reach a customer, win trust and make an offer? The Red Pill changes what can be supplied. Instead of selling only an agency engagement or a product, someone contributes to a web of reusable intelligence, checked by people with specific skills.\nThat would be a different economy of exchange. The valuable token would be intelligence itself. Calling it a token here means a proposed separable unit of capability; it does not establish that a blockchain token, a payment instrument or a market already makes that unit possible.\nEarly enough to build\nIf this is the direction of travel, it is not too late. It may be as early as it could be.\nThink of businesses collecting data in the early 2000s and 2010s, before their analytical tools could make full use of it. Some of that accumulated history became useful when the tools improved. My suggestion follows the same pattern: build and retain operational intelligence while we work out how to make it transferable. Know what the operation does, where it fails, what the skilled person notices and what makes a result acceptable.\nIn this scenario, the more specific and separable the useful piece, the sooner someone else can adopt it. A capability that solves an identifiable problem has a place in a network of intelligence. That is what I imagine becoming practical AI for everyone. It is a hypothesis about adoption, not a guarantee that narrowness by itself creates demand or income.\nI'll admit it sounds like a fairy tale: free markets and human alignment magically cooperate, freeing us from the shackles of whatever, blah blah blah. There is a lot of work between the premise and that world. We still have to make the pieces transferable, make the quality assurance meaningful and make the exchange reward the people doing it.\nBut hey, it beats the Blue Pill scenario anyway.\nJust remember: objects in the mirror are closer than they appear.\nThe later Cheap copies, scarce attention follows the market for easier production and harder selection. One Yardstick, One Clock turns to a testable comparison of human and agent execution, and asks who can buy what the resulting economy produces. Those are ways to work on the questions this scenario opens.\nMcKinsey Global Institute, Jobs lost, jobs gained, November 2017, reports the 400–800 million range for its midpoint through rapid automation-adoption scenarios, with occupational transitions and job creation considered separately. Goldman Sachs, Generative AI could raise global GDP by 7%, April 2023, describes exposure of work equivalent to 300 million full-time jobs and explicitly distinguishes exposure from layoffs. These are projections under different assumptions, not interchangeable counts of observed displacement.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/the-red-pill/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/","identifier":"content:post-agi-economics-collections","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Thoughts","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":1,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-core-framework","name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap"}}]},"description":"What changes in an economy, and in the models that describe it, when thinking gets cheap: where the constraint moves, who gets the gain and what counts as evidence. Essays that stand alone are on the Blog.","text":"","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-core-framework","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"description":"Follow production, scarcity and whether familiar economic models still hold as cognitive work becomes cheaper.","text":"How much of our account of an economy depends on thinking remaining costly? Wages, prices, productivity and consumption are connected through ratios and rates. Let the cost of cognition tend towards zero and its available supply towards infinity. Which relationships survive that limit, and which would we have to work out again?\nThe answer depends on what we want to explain. A production model may still tell us how much can be made while leaving open who receives the income or can buy the output. Two purchases can cost the same and leave the buyer with different possibilities. An accurate total can therefore be enough for one question and insufficient for the next.\nFollowing those consequences tells us what to keep in the account. Sometimes the existing relations can describe the change once their values are recalculated. Sometimes we need to distinguish people, decisions or connections that the model left out. The reason to make it richer comes from the question it cannot yet answer.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/","identifier":"journey:post-agi-abundance-and-description","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From abundance to a changed description","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-models-measures","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Models and measures","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-production-income","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Who gets the gain","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-scarcity-value","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Where the constraint moves","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/","identifier":"journey:post-agi-abundance-and-description","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From abundance to a changed description","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From abundance to a changed description","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/"}}]}],"description":"Follow cheap cognition through a surviving production model, the destination of a saving and the point where a richer account becomes necessary.","text":"What does an economic comparison hold fixed? A production technology can stay the same while the price, residual income and recipient change, and expenditure can stay the same while two purchases cease to fit together. A claim that cognition becomes abundant raises three questions in order. What does an existing production model predict? Who receives the resulting saving? And when does the description need to change to explain a consequence it previously left out?\nThe first article derives a small production example and shows why ownership matters even when total output is unchanged. The second follows a thirty-unit cost saving through different price and income arrangements. The third returns to a workshop and two purchasing decisions to distinguish a changed parameter, a moving constraint and information an aggregate cannot retain. The sequence moves from recalculating an answer to identifying what a new account must preserve.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-core-framework","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}],"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/when-thinking-stops-being-scarce/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-when-thinking-stops-being-scarce","@type":"Article","name":"When thinking stops being the scarce part","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/when-thinking-stops-being-scarce/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/premises/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-premises","@type":"Article","name":"Where does the saving go?","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/premises/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/transition-dynamics/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-transition-dynamics","@type":"Article","name":"What changes when the constraint moves?","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/transition-dynamics/"}}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/premises/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-premises","@type":"Article","name":"Where does the saving go?","headline":"Where does the saving go?","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["AI cost savings","price pass-through","distribution of gains","fixed-price contracts","wages"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/premises/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Where does the saving go?","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/premises/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From abundance to a changed description","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Where does the saving go?","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/premises/"}}]}]},"description":"A service that cost 100 now costs 70. Does the customer pay less or the supplier keep more, and whose income did the thirty used to be?","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-10-07","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Where does the saving go?\nA service used to cost 100 to produce and now costs 70. Thirty units have become available. Does the customer pay less, does the supplier earn more, or does the saving finance another round of work?\nPut a price beside the cost. Before the improvement, one unit sells for 120, costs 100 and leaves 20 with the supplier. Hold quantity and quality fixed for a moment. The new production cost is 70, but that number alone does not determine the new selling price.\nThirty units, two sides of the transaction\nLet p denote the price after the improvement. The customer's saving on the same purchase is 120-p. The supplier's residual rises from 20 to p-70, a gain of p-90. Together:\n\n\\underbrace{(120-p)}_{\\text{customer saving}}\n+\n\\underbrace{(p-90)}_{\\text{supplier's additional residual}}\n=30.\n\nThe units are hypothetical money amounts for one unchanged service, not measured AI savings. Taxes, financing and additional fixed costs are left outside this first comparison.\nPrice after the improvement\tProduction cost\tSupplier's residual\tCustomer saving\tIncrease in supplier's residual\t\n120\t70\t50\t0\t30\t\n105\t70\t35\t15\t15\t\n90\t70\t20\t30\t0\t\nEach row contains the same technology. What differs is the price arrangement. A fixed-price contract leaves the saving initially with the supplier. Full pass-through preserves its old residual and lowers the customer's bill. The middle row shares the immediate benefit.\nCompetition, bargaining and contractual terms help explain which row resembles an actual transaction. They cannot be inferred from the cost reduction. Nor does the supplier's residual tell us which person ultimately receives it: the business could distribute it, invest it or use it to meet another obligation.\nThe cost also belonged to someone\nThe table looks different from the other side of the old expenditure. If all thirty units were wages removed from this service, a worker loses thirty while the transaction becomes cheaper to supply. In the first row the supplier gains that amount. In the last row customers gain it through their lower bills. The former worker need not be the customer receiving the saving.\nIf instead the improvement reduces electricity use or rework, another set of payments and resources changes. The same cost number can conceal different income effects. That is why a local gain and a household gain require connected accounts, rather than a presumption that the saving reaches everyone equally. Income and prices meet inside the household's budget.\nThe first transaction is not the whole adjustment\nA lower price can bring more customers. A higher residual can fund capacity. Work released from this service can become useful elsewhere, or remain unused. Once quantity responds, thirty units per service is no longer the total gain; once the workflow changes, the cost may include new checking and integration.\nEven the quality held fixed here has to survive contact with the task. A faster draft that requires expensive correction may not cost 70 when the complete job is counted. The execution comparison has to establish that part before the economic allocation begins.\nThis small account keeps the question concrete. We know what the technology changed, what the price rule held fixed and where the immediate difference appeared. To compare worker ownership, public provision or a longer investment path, we need the arrangement around that transaction. The saving has a size. Its destination still has to be explained.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/premises/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/","identifier":"journey:post-agi-abundance-and-description","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From abundance to a changed description","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/transition-dynamics/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-transition-dynamics","@type":"Article","name":"What changes when the constraint moves?","headline":"What changes when the constraint moves?","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["moving constraints","aggregation and information loss","model closure","ownership rules","survey definition changes","singular limits"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/transition-dynamics/","@type":"WebPage","name":"What changes when the constraint moves?","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/transition-dynamics/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From abundance to a changed description","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"What changes when the constraint moves?","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/transition-dynamics/"}}]}]},"description":"A workshop can run out of machine time, change owners or lose track of a necessary part. Each change asks something different of its economic model.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-10-14","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"What changes when the constraint moves?\nA workshop needs usable plans and machine time. When plans are scarce, producing them faster can raise output. Once enough plans exist, the machines become the limit. Another plan then adds little to current production, even though its cost has fallen. A production relation that represents both inputs can describe the workshop on either side of this crossing. The answer changes because the limiting input changes. But suppose the workshop changes owners, delegates its purchases or stops recording a distinction we need. Recalculating its output alone will not tell us what those changes do.\nRe-solving the production problem\nA lower price changes the relative cost of inputs; new capacity changes the feasible set. The constraint can move from planning to machinery, drafting to approval, or producing copies to reaching an audience. Even a sharp change in the solution can be something the model was built to describe. The workshop's outcome depends on the relation among its inputs. With strict complements, unused plans wait for machine time. With substitution, a cheaper input may replace another. A planning improvement could also reduce the machine time needed per job, changing a coefficient rather than simply adding plans. These possibilities call for different accounts of the improvement. Re-solving a model that includes the relevant relation can be enough; the scale of the technology's ambition does not decide whether new formal machinery is necessary.\nA decision inside the aggregate\nSuppose a firm delegates purchases to two systems. Treating the firm as one decision-maker may remain adequate for total expenditure. It may be inadequate for the compatibility of what was purchased, the permission to buy it or responsibility for an error. Two purchasing systems could each respect a spending limit while jointly ordering incompatible inputs. Another pair could spend the same aggregate amount on a compatible set. The total records expenditure in both cases but loses the relation that determines whether production can proceed. Representing the separate decisions and their coordination becomes useful for that question.\nMake the lost distinction explicit. Suppose the systems each buy one component for fifteen units. Components come in two interface types; a pair works only when the types match.\nPurchases\tTotal expenditure\tCan the components work together?\t\nType A and type A\t30\tYes\t\nType A and type B\t30\tNo\t\nWrite the coarse observation as g(x)=30 in both cases, and compatibility as h(x), equal to one for the first pair and zero for the second. No function of expenditure alone can recover compatibility: it would have to assign both values to the same input. Retaining the two interface types restores the answer in this finite example.\nThe aggregate still answers the expenditure question. It fails only when asked for the relation it discarded. This elementary loss of information is a useful diagnostic; a general result about when an economic model must be enlarged would require a specified class of models and permitted observations, not just this table.\nWho owns the gain is set in the model's closure\nThe workshop might change who receives its residual income while leaving the production technology untouched. Private ownership, a worker-held claim and a shared public pool can give the same output gain different income and demand consequences. A model with an adjustable ownership rule can compare these arrangements. One that fixes ownership implicitly needs a changed closure before it can answer the same question. The closure specifies what adjusts, what stays fixed, who receives the gain and who bears the loss. It is a different part of the argument from the production relation.\nBehaviour can then change in response to the arrangement. People learn, firms enter and customers respond to incentives. A coefficient estimated under the previous conditions may cease to describe the new ones. Re-specifying or re-estimating that relation is the answer when behaviour has changed the relation itself; accounting identities can remain true throughout.\nWhen the thing counted and the way it is counted both change\nA survey broadening its definition of AI use can create a jump in a reported series even if no firm changes its workflow that day. The event counted by an adoption rate has moved. Comparability requires a common counted event or a visible break in the series.\nIdentification poses another problem. Firms adopting a tool may already differ in management, capacity or demand. The observed productivity gap includes those prior differences unless a suitable comparison separates them from the effect of adoption. A meaningful economic object can therefore remain difficult to identify with the available data. The repair follows the problem: a comparable measure, a stronger research design or a narrower estimable claim. Categories and incentives also shape the observation. A changed category can reveal an existing activity, while a reward attached to the measure can change the activity that follows.\nWhen a price approaching zero changes the model itself\nAs a price approaches zero, a model may continue to describe the activity at increasingly extreme values. A singular limit is more specific: rank, state dimension, regularity or solution class can change, or solving the model and then taking its limit can give a different answer from taking the limit first. The distinction comes from a derivation. It gives the research a way to investigate an actual change in the problem instead of using abundance as a substitute for one. A large economic change and a formal singularity can coincide, but each needs its own argument.\nEach frontier moves the others\nCapability, affordable access, reliable operation, organisational adoption and institutional response move through different processes. Permission can enable operation; operation can expose a bottleneck; investment can remove it; lower cost can alter demand. The order of crossings feeds back into their timing. A clock for one of these frontiers needs a defined process and an account of the conditions under which the crossing becomes possible; one universal countdown would erase the dependencies that make those times different.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/transition-dynamics/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/","identifier":"journey:post-agi-abundance-and-description","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From abundance to a changed description","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/when-thinking-stops-being-scarce/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-when-thinking-stops-being-scarce","@type":"Article","name":"When thinking stops being the scarce part","headline":"When thinking stops being the scarce part","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["Post-AGI Economics","cognitive abundance","complementary inputs","constant-elasticity production function","factor income shares","ownership and demand","economic model assumptions"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/when-thinking-stops-being-scarce/","@type":"WebPage","name":"When thinking stops being the scarce part","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/when-thinking-stops-being-scarce/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From abundance to a changed description","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"When thinking stops being the scarce part","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/when-thinking-stops-being-scarce/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/productivity-and-purchasing-power/","https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-clock-many-frontiers/"]},"description":"Cheap cognition can leave a production model intact while moving income to the scarce complement. A worked example asks what the aggregate then leaves out.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-09-23","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"When thinking stops being the scarce part\nPush the cost of cognition towards zero and its available supply towards infinity. What happens to wages and productivity, prices and output, income and consumption, money and its circulation? Economics describes these relationships through ratios, rates and models built for particular conditions. My starting question is what happens when a numerator or denominator approaches an extreme that those conditions never allowed. Which relationships would we have to work out again?\nThink of a factory that can produce as many designs as it wants. It still needs energy, equipment and materials to make them. Abundant design may leave its production model intact while moving the constraint to another input. But knowing how much the factory can produce does not yet tell us who receives the income or who can afford its output.\nThose are two different ways for the inquiry to develop. We can follow a model into the limit and find that it keeps answering its question. We can also discover that an answer about output leaves out a relationship we need to understand income, purchasing power or the time people have to adapt. Start with the assumptions, then see which kind of change we actually have.\nWhich assumption moved?\nAn economic model does not carry every assumption made by the discipline. Its own account has to be examined. Does it depend on scarce skilled judgment, costly search, slow contracting or limited coordination? Does it hold the firm, the employment relationship or the owner of an asset fixed? Does it use a market price to represent an output that might become free, personalised or produced inside a household? Does its estimate depend on behaviour and institutions staying stable long enough to compare?\nCheap cognition can put pressure on each of those assumptions without changing them all at once. A machine can draft a contract quickly while the parties still need time to negotiate it. A design can be copied cheaply while permission to manufacture it remains scarce. A firm can automate a task while leaving its contracts, customers and ownership unchanged. The model needs the particular change that matters to its question.\nA new parameter value may call for recalculation. If a different input becomes scarce, we need to follow that input and its income. If an actor or relation is missing, we need to add it to the account. Each changes what we have to do next; taking a quantity to an extreme is how we investigate the model, not a reason to decide the result beforehand.\nMore cognition, the same complement\nTake a deliberately small production model. Let C>0 be cognitive input and P>0 an essential complement. In this example, output is\n\nY(C,P)=\\frac{2CP}{C+P}.\n\nThis constant-elasticity production function has equal weights and elasticity 1/2. Choose units for the two inputs within this model: C represents cognitive input and P the particular complement being examined. Applying it to an industry would require defining and measuring those inputs.\nHold P fixed and increase C. Dividing the numerator and denominator by C gives\n\nY(C,P)=\\frac{2P}{1+P/C}\\longrightarrow 2P.\n\nMore cognition still helps, but it cannot replace the complement. At output price one, the marginal values are\n\n\\frac{\\partial Y}{\\partial C}=\\frac{2P^2}{(C+P)^2},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial Y}{\\partial P}=\\frac{2C^2}{(C+P)^2}.\n\nAs cognition becomes abundant, the marginal value of another unit of C approaches zero. The marginal value of another unit of P approaches two. This does not say that thinking is useless: without it the joint production fails. It says that the next unit of an already abundant input contributes less than the next unit of the scarce complement.\nUnder competitive marginal-product payments, the complement's income share is\n\n\\frac{P\\,\\partial Y/\\partial P}{Y}=\\frac{C}{C+P}\\longrightarrow 1.\n\nThe production relation has not broken. It tells us where income moves under its assumptions. Ownership tells us who receives it. A different bargaining or payment rule would require a different calculation. The workshop example follows the same shift with strict complements: once plans are plentiful, machine time limits the next job. Here the smooth production relation also lets us follow the marginal payments.\nFor P=1, the progression is:\nWith the complement fixed at one, output rises toward two as cognitive input increases, its marginal contribution falls toward zero, and the complement's income share rises toward one.\nAt C=1,3,9, output is 1,3/2,9/5; cognition's marginal value is 1/2,1/8,1/50; and the complement's income share is 1/2,3/4,9/10. The three panels trace those same formulas between the selected points. Income shares assume competitive marginal-product payments at output price one.\nThe same output can reach different buyers\nNow hold the production model, quantities and payment rule fixed. Set C=3 and P=1. Output is 3/2. The cognitive input receives 3/8 and the complement receives 9/8; together those payments exhaust the output.\nSuppose the recipient of the cognitive payment stays the same. Compare two possible owners of the complement. One household spends 3/4 of the income it receives; the other spends 1/4. Those spending fractions are stipulated for this example. They are not estimates of actual households, and the example does not yet explain saving, prices or a subsequent equilibrium.\nSwitching the recipient of the complement's payment changes consumption by\n\n\\left(\\frac34-\\frac14\\right)\\frac98=\\frac9{16}.\n\nThe inputs, output and total factor payments are identical in the two cases. An account retaining only those aggregates therefore cannot distinguish them. Whatever consumption it predicts from that account, it must return the same answer twice. Since the actual answers are 9/16 apart, it misses by at least half that gap, 9/32, in one case. Recording who owns the complement and how that recipient spends recovers the missing distinction.\nThis is a small version of the question studied in Compression is a promise about questions. Two situations can look identical through one description and differ in a consequence we care about. Here ordinary ownership and consumption accounting supplies the repair.\nIt does make the buyer question unavoidable for this comparison. More output and more household purchasing power are different claims. Who gets to buy what machines produce? follows the channels through which people might receive the gains: prices, wages, new work, ownership, transfers and public demand. A channel must be described and tested before its existence is treated as compensation for another channel's loss.\nWhen, how and what belong together\nA calculation at two values of C leaves out the transition between them. Who invests before the complement becomes valuable? Who owns it already? Which temporary businesses appear because deployment is incomplete? Can a firm reverse its choice, and can a household wait for the next source of income? Those questions change which future becomes reachable.\nThere is no single morning on which every task and institution becomes post-AGI. Capability, access, recurring use and institutional adaptation can cross their thresholds at different times. One Yardstick, One Clock asks how comparable execution and a model of transition could make the time available to adapt measurable. That clock remains an instrument to develop and test. The inquiry here develops alongside it: where do constraints and income move, and which description keeps explaining the change?\nThe history of media makes the interaction easier to see. Radio became Spotify follows a need that persists while distribution, habits, measurements and businesses change around it. A catalogue still needs an audience asks which scarce resource earns income after copying becomes cheap. These histories supply mechanisms to investigate. They do not settle an AGI date or prove that the same ownership and demand arrangements will recur.\nWhat would make the example useful outside the page?\nThe next empirical step needs a named production setting, meaningful measures of cognition and its complements, evidence for substitution, a payment rule and observations of who receives and spends the income. Alternative explanations need the same opportunity to fit. If a conventional model answers the question, that is useful progress. If it fails, the failure must say which consequence it lost and what additional information restores it.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/when-thinking-stops-being-scarce/cognition-and-the-complement.svg","description":"With the complement fixed at one, output rises toward two as cognitive input increases, its marginal contribution falls toward zero, and the complement's income share rises toward one.","caption":"At C=1,3,9, output is 1,3/2,9/5; cognition's marginal value is 1/2,1/8,1/50; and the complement's income share is 1/2,3/4,9/10. The three panels trace those same formulas between the selected points. Income shares assume competitive marginal-product payments at output price one.\n"}],"url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/when-thinking-stops-being-scarce/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/","identifier":"journey:post-agi-abundance-and-description","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From abundance to a changed description","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-models-measures","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Models and measures","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Models and measures","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/"}}]}],"description":"What a count of AI use actually counts, and how a forecast is checked against the event it named.","text":"How much AI is in the economy? A count of firms using a tool and a count of completed autonomous tasks give different answers because they count different things. Money spent on software asks another question again.\nThe economic question gives a counted event its meaning. A count which once located a new activity can become uninformative when that activity spreads into tasks the survey never distinguished. A revised definition can then improve the observation while breaking comparison with earlier years. Understanding that trade-off matters before the series becomes evidence about production, or an input to a clock.\nThe definition also matters when a forecast meets its outcome. If a prediction concerns recurring use but the later survey counts any trial, a rise in the number does not settle the prediction. Across enough comparable cases, with each prediction’s original event and information preserved, we can ask whether the model separates cases with different chances of success, and whether outcomes occur about as often as it predicts. A forecast has something to answer to when we can tell what happened without changing what it said.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-core-framework","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}],"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/a-forecast-meets-its-outcome/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-forecast-evaluation","@type":"TechArticle","name":"A forecast meets its outcome","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/a-forecast-meets-its-outcome/"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/measuring-ai-diffusion/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-measuring-diffusion","@type":"Article","name":"Counting the spread of AI","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/measuring-ai-diffusion/"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/metrics-are-models/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-metrics-models","@type":"Article","name":"The economy inside the measure","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/metrics-are-models/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/a-forecast-meets-its-outcome/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-forecast-evaluation","@type":"TechArticle","name":"A forecast meets its outcome","headline":"A forecast meets its outcome","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["forecast evaluation","Brier score","logarithmic score","calibration","rolling-origin comparison"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/a-forecast-meets-its-outcome/","@type":"WebPage","name":"A forecast meets its outcome","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/a-forecast-meets-its-outcome/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Models and measures","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"A forecast meets its outcome","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/a-forecast-meets-its-outcome/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-clock-many-frontiers/","https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/evaluation-and-execution/","https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/measuring-ai-diffusion/"]},"description":"A forecast issued before an outcome earns trust through calibration, informative probabilities and a fair baseline.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-09-22","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"A forecast meets its outcome\nAt the end of June, a model assigns probability 0.8 to an event in September: half of a stated group of firms will operate a particular invoice-preparation workflow at the required quality. September arrives. How should that forecast meet what happened?\nThe example is hypothetical, but it gives the timing problem something to answer to. A probability of 0.8 is neither a guarantee nor a way to remain right regardless of the result. It is a distribution whose confidence has consequences when scored. A useful One Yardstick clock would earn trust through such encounters: an event defined before its outcome, information available at the time, and a comparison with what a simpler forecast could say.\nThe prediction carries a date\nThe population, workflow and acceptance condition are part of the event; September and the June information cutoff are part of the prediction. Another forecast made in August can use August information. It answers the same target event from a different starting position and shorter horizon. An August model cannot be credited with a June prediction by fitting to the completed quarter afterwards. Similarly, a revised historical series may support a better present model while remaining unavailable to the original forecaster at the time. This is why a forecast needs an information vintage as well as a model version. When a forecast is issued, it can use only what could then be known. A later outcome can correct the observation; it cannot silently improve the earlier information set.\nA forecast record separates what was known from what was later observed:\nRecord\tOrigin and information cutoff\tTarget\tProbability or outcome\t\nFirst forecast\tEnd of June\tStated September event\t0.8\t\nUpdated forecast\tEnd of August\tThe same September event\tIts newly issued probability\t\nOutcome\tEnd of September\tThe original workflow, population and acceptance condition\tObserved yes or no, or an explicitly missing observation\t\nThe August update gets to learn from August. It does not replace the June record. A missing September observation is also different from a non-occurrence.\nA probability is more than a yes or a no\nAn event assigned probability 0.8 can fail to happen without logically contradicting the forecast. The remaining 0.2 is part of what was predicted. Yet a forecaster assigning 0.8 to everything should not be able to claim perpetual success by invoking that possibility after every failure.\nScoring makes the whole probability consequential. For a binary event with outcome y\\in\\{0,1\\}, the Brier loss is:\n\nL_{\\mathrm{Brier}}(p,y)=(p-y)^2.\n\nLower is better. If the event happens, p = 0.8 receives loss 0.04 and p = 0.5 receives 0.25. If it does not happen, the losses are 0.64 and 0.25. Confidence creates both the possibility of a better score and a larger penalty for being wrong.\nThe logarithmic loss makes another trade-off:\n\nL_{\\log}(p,y)=-y\\log p-(1-y)\\log(1-p).\n\nFor an observed event use -\\log p; for a non-event use -\\log(1-p). A certain prediction has zero loss if it is right and infinite loss if it is wrong. Assigning zero probability to an outcome that occurs is therefore penalised without bound. Gneiting and Raftery's treatment of proper scoring rules explains why a forecaster minimises its expected loss by reporting its assessed distribution honestly.\nCalibration needs resolution\nIn a constructed ten-case example, forecasts consist of five assigned probability 0.8 and five assigned 0.2. Four events occur in the first group and one in the second. The observed frequencies equal the predicted probabilities in these small groups, and the mean Brier loss is 0.16. A constant 0.5 forecast on these same outcomes receives 0.25. The constant forecast gets the overall frequency right: five of ten events occurred. But it makes no distinction between cases. The varying probabilities preserve useful resolution while matching the group frequencies.\nAll three calibration points match their group frequencies, but the varying forecast has mean Brier loss 0.16 against 0.25 for the constant forecast.\nThe counts belong beside the calibration points: there are only five cases in each varying-probability group, and ten in the constant comparison. These exact proportions explain the example; they do not estimate stable calibration from a large evaluation.\nTo judge calibration beyond this example, we need enough relevant cases and an account of their uncertainty and dependence. Ten forecasts about the same sector and overlapping quarter are not ten independent replications. A system can also score well overall while overestimating one group's prospects or failing on a changed operating condition. Which cases go wrong matters when someone uses the forecast.\nThe alternative can be very simple\nA sophisticated timing model has to add information beyond a simpler comparator. For an adoption event, a baseline might use the previous observed rate or a predeclared trend. For a crossing time, it might use a fixed hazard under stated conditions. The baseline should be credible for the question, not intentionally bad. Both forecasts need the same event, origins, horizons and observations. One model predicting three months ahead cannot be compared casually with another updated the day before the outcome. Trying many model variants on a small evaluation window can also select a lucky winner. A rolling-origin comparison, in which each origin uses only prior information, makes the prospective test more faithful, though overlapping outcomes and structural change still affect its interpretation.\nA forecast of the waiting time also has to account for incomplete observation. If the frontier has not been crossed by the end of observation, the waiting time is censored: it is known to exceed the observed interval, not to equal it. A score for a fully observed time cannot simply substitute the censoring date as an event date. Event-by-horizon probabilities remain one possible local target, provided the chosen horizons and observation rules are explicit.\nA changed question starts another comparison\nSuppose the original target counted recurring operation, but a later survey counts any trial of an assistant. The measured series can rise even when recurring operation has not changed. The counted event has changed. It cannot be treated as a favourable outcome for the original forecast without a defensible bridge between definitions. A model update can legitimately improve future forecasts. A changed target can legitimately ask a better question. Keeping both histories distinct reveals what improved: the predictive method, the available information or the thing being predicted. It also reveals a forecast that no longer has a comparable outcome, rather than converting uncertainty into an automatic win.\nA local economic clock gives a particular crossing a mathematical process. Its forecast then has to meet that process in public: definite enough to be scored, informative enough to beat a simpler account, and revisable without rewriting what it previously said. Trust accumulates through those encounters, not through the elegance of the countdown.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/a-forecast-meets-its-outcome/calibration-and-resolution.svg","description":"All three calibration points match their group frequencies, but the varying forecast has mean Brier loss 0.16 against 0.25 for the constant forecast.","caption":"The counts belong beside the calibration points: there are only five cases in each varying-probability group, and ten in the constant comparison. These exact proportions explain the example; they do not estimate stable calibration from a large evaluation.\n"}],"url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/a-forecast-meets-its-outcome/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-models-measures","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Models and measures","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/measuring-ai-diffusion/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-measuring-diffusion","@type":"Article","name":"Counting the spread of AI","headline":"Counting the spread of AI","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["AI adoption","business surveys","Business Trends and Outlook Survey","measurement comparability","adoption denominators","selection effects"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/measuring-ai-diffusion/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Counting the spread of AI","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/measuring-ai-diffusion/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Models and measures","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Counting the spread of AI","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/measuring-ai-diffusion/"}}]}]},"description":"A broader survey category can reveal more AI use without showing a matching change in production.","datePublished":{"@value":"2026-06-01","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Counting the spread of AI\nImagine that an adoption survey broadens \"AI used in production\" to \"AI used in any business function\". A firm drafting an internal email now qualifies. Its reported category can change while its production process stays exactly as it was.\nThe broader question reveals uses that were previously invisible and changes what can be compared over time. An increase can come from firms changing how they work, the survey counting more kinds of work, or both. Which event the survey counts, whom it asks and when it asks them all shape the answer.\nThe question defines the counted event\nThe US Census Bureau made this change in its Business Trends and Outlook Survey in November 2025, broadening the question from AI used in producing goods or services to AI used in any business function. Its May 2026 account also distinguishes recent use from expected future use. The wording change creates a break in what the historical series counts. Comparing the periods requires a common definition where one can be recovered, and a visible break where it cannot. A better current observation can therefore make a simple historical comparison less reliable.\nA firm is not a unit of work\nSuppose, hypothetically, ten small firms each use one assistant occasionally and one large firm delegates a substantial recurring workflow. A firm-count adoption rate treats each adopter as one. A worker-weighted rate, a task-time rate and a value-added rate can tell different stories. None is inherently the correct rate for every question. Firm counts describe diffusion across firms. Worker weights describe reach across people. A task denominator can describe coverage of work, but requires a definition of tasks and a treatment of assistance versus autonomy. Value weights ask a different question again. A number cannot become the automation share in a production model merely because it is the only frequent series available.\nConsider a small constructed survey, separate from the Census observations. All ten responding firms remain unchanged between two questionnaires:\nFirm category\tFirms\tQualify as using AI in production\tQualify as using AI in any function\t\nRecurring production use\t2\t2\t2\t\nInternal email assistance only\t3\t0\t3\t\nNo use\t5\t0\t0\t\nTotal\t10\t2\t5\t\nThe measured rate rises from 20 to 50 percent solely because the event changes. A bridge questionnaire asking both questions could locate the additional uses. It would not justify multiplying all earlier production-use rates by 2.5: the overlap can change across populations and dates.\nExpectations, access and use are different observations\nAn executive's plan to adopt is not observed adoption. Access to a tool does not show that people use it. Recent use does not show that it completes work at the required quality. Self-reported time saved does not show where the saving went. Yotzov and coauthors' 2026 executive survey separately asks about current use, reported past effects and expected future effects. Those categories can disagree without any respondent being inconsistent: firms may expect a larger change than they have experienced. An adoption model that blends them into one state loses that information. It may also confuse anticipation with the effect of deployed technology.\nSelection can look like a treatment effect\nFirms that adopt may already have better data, more skilled workers or stronger demand. Workers using a tool may be those most able to benefit from it. The difference between users and non-users then includes more than the tool. A survey can describe that selection. It cannot automatically remove it. A causal comparison needs a design whose assumptions address why adoption occurred, alongside a clear definition of the treatment and outcome.\nThe same caution applies to task exposure. An occupation-level score crosswalked to an industry is several steps away from observed autonomous work in a particular firm. It may be informative, but its grain and intended use should remain visible.\nKeeping a series comparable as use changes\nA consistent series can follow a local frontier: a defined kind of use among a stated population over time. A survey showing widespread assistance answers a useful question while leaving the coverage of autonomous execution open. Preserving both conclusions makes the evidence available to the worker-outcome inquiry without turning use into an employment effect. A local clock likewise needs a frontier observed consistently enough to time. Definitions and denominators determine which movement the series can describe. They are part of the economic account, not footnotes beneath its headline number.\nThe Business Trends and Outlook Survey covers US employer businesses, excluding farms. It asks about AI use during the preceding two weeks and expected use over the next six months. The Census Bureau's May 2026 account examines responses collected from 14 December 2025 to 3 May 2026, after the wording changed on 17 November 2025. Its use rates count businesses, not the share of their work performed by AI.\nThe March 2026 revision of Firm Data on AI draws on nearly 6,000 senior executives reporting about their firms in the US, UK, Germany and Australia between November 2025 and January 2026. It asks which AI technologies the firm uses or intends to use, and separately asks about effects on employment and sales volume per employee over the previous three years and expected effects over the next three. Those effects are executives' reports and expectations.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/measuring-ai-diffusion/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-models-measures","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Models and measures","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/metrics-are-models/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-metrics-models","@type":"Article","name":"The economy inside the measure","headline":"The economy inside the measure","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["measuring AI in national accounts","value added","quality adjustment","economic measurement","welfare","metric incentives"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/metrics-are-models/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The economy inside the measure","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/metrics-are-models/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Models and measures","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"The economy inside the measure","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/metrics-are-models/"}}]}]},"description":"Counting AI production, AI use and welfare requires different observation and accounting boundaries.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-02-24","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The economy inside the measure\nA service answers twice as many questions at half the price. Its customers spend the same total amount. Has the economy gained a larger service, merely produced more text, or moved effort into correcting the answers?\nIn this hypothetical comparison, expenditure counts payments, answer counts track output, and useful service asks whether the answer helped the person who requested it. As AI changes production, an increase in one measure can come from a change in the work or from a change in which parts of the work are counted.\nProducing AI and producing with it\nA supplier builds software, a factory uses it and a customer buys the factory's output. The software is an input to the factory's production. Adding the gross sales at every layer would count some of the same activity more than once. The Bureau of Economic Analysis' January 2025 study distinguishes AI production from its uses within national accounting. It considers a thematic satellite account spanning manufacturing, software, data services and research, with classification and data limitations. Locating the technology in an existing accounting system is substantive work: the categories determine where its contribution appears, rather than making GDP meaningless simply because the technology is new.\nHere is a deliberately stripped-down supply chain, with no imports or other intermediate purchases:\nProducer\tOutput sold\tIntermediate purchases\tValue added\t\nSoftware supplier\t20\t0\t20\t\nFactory buying that software\t100\t20\t80\t\nCombined\t120 in gross sales\t20\t100\t\nThe customer buys the factory's final output for 100. Adding 120 in gross sales would count the software once as the supplier's output and again inside the factory's price. Subtracting intermediate use gives 100 of value added. In this example, both the cost and the supplier's output concern software consumed in the period; a capitalised asset would require a different accounting treatment. The example explains the boundary, not a proposed AI satellite account.\nQuantity, quality and welfare\nReturn to the service that produces twice as many answers at half the price. Nominal expenditure can remain unchanged. If the answers are equally useful, more service is available; if many need correction, an answer count overstates useful output. New users gaining access introduce a welfare question extending beyond the expenditure total. Quality adjustment depends on purpose. Accuracy, timeliness, accessibility and consequential errors measure different aspects of a service. A price index cannot settle their social value by quietly assigning them one weight. Likewise, a free output may create value, impose costs, replace a paid service or consume attention. Accounting and welfare require connected arguments with different boundaries.\nOperations, jobs and outcomes\nCompute utilisation compares performed operations with a capacity benchmark. A completed-job measure follows a workflow; revenue follows transactions; an outcome measure follows the result someone needs. Their relationships depend on the workload and the process between them. A provider could raise utilisation by accepting less valuable work. It could reduce average job cost while making important jobs wait longer. A team could produce more drafts while finishing fewer accepted documents. In each case the convenient number improves while another consequence deteriorates. A useful comparison therefore carries the workload mix, denominator, target and relevant constraints with it. Where quality changes, the comparison needs to show that change rather than silently treating every unit as equivalent.\nThe incentive inside the observation\nWhen funding or permission depends on a metric, participants respond to it. A delivered-capacity measure can encourage completion while neglecting whom the service reaches. A count of drafts can encourage drafting at the expense of review. Sometimes that response is the intended incentive; sometimes it moves work away from the purpose the measure was selected to serve. Additional measures can make a trade-off visible, but they also cost effort to collect. Combining them into a single index introduces a choice about their relative importance. The observation rule is consequently part of the institutional arrangement, not a neutral window onto it.\nA repair might change the denominator, preserve a separate quality boundary, follow a linked outcome or decline to aggregate. Broadening the event counted as AI adoption can raise a reported rate without changing a single firm's activity. Repairing that comparison requires a common counted event or a visible break in the series. A changed production relation poses a different problem: the process itself has changed, not just the way it was counted.\nThe measure remains useful when its interpretation can travel with the number: what activity it counts, for which question, under which definition. That is how an account can evolve with the work without losing the consequence it was meant to reveal.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/metrics-are-models/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-models-measures","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Models and measures","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-scarcity-value","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Where the constraint moves","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Where the constraint moves","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/"}}]}],"description":"Where complements bind, who controls access and how long a premium lasts.","text":"The next scarce thing can command attention without commanding all the income attributed to it. A necessary connection may be supplied competitively, reserved by contract or controlled exclusively. Each arrangement changes the bargain around the same physical contribution.\nA claim on value depends on alternatives: who can reach another provider, how long expansion takes and which rights permit access. The ability to charge for a bottleneck is therefore an economic relationship to explain, not a property that follows automatically from its technical importance.\nThe horizon joins those questions. A high price can finance new supply, and new supply can remove the premium. A useful bridge need not remain a toll road.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-core-framework","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}],"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/capital-theory/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-capital","@type":"Article","name":"After the planning bottleneck","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/capital-theory/"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/scarcity-and-rent/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-scarcity-rent","@type":"Article","name":"Who captures value when the bottleneck moves?","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/scarcity-and-rent/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/capital-theory/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-capital","@type":"Article","name":"After the planning bottleneck","headline":"After the planning bottleneck","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["complementary inputs","strict complements","production bottlenecks","moving constraints","task-based productivity","economic rent"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/capital-theory/","@type":"WebPage","name":"After the planning bottleneck","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/capital-theory/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Where the constraint moves","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"After the planning bottleneck","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/capital-theory/"}}]}]},"description":"More plans make a workshop's machine time binding; complementary inputs explain the next useful investment.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-01-20","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"After the planning bottleneck\nA workshop has five usable plans and machine time for ten jobs. Better planning gives it twenty plans, but the machines still carry only ten. The workshop has gained a real capability, and its next useful investment has changed.\nThis deliberately hypothetical picture is a way into the economics of cheaper cognition. Plans, designs and coordination can become easier to supply while equipment, energy, access or human presence still bounds the result. Following the constraint lets us ask what expands, who can supply the next missing input and which account of production continues to describe the activity.\nA workshop limited by planning and by machine time\nGive each completed job one unit of planning and one unit of machine time. The workshop's capacity is then the smaller of its planning capacity and machine capacity. With five planning units and ten machine units, it can complete five jobs. Raising planning to twenty permits ten, not twenty. The same relation describes both cases. At first an extra planning unit can help; once the machine is full, more planning cannot increase output unless it improves the process or frees machine time. A falling price of planning does not erase the machine constraint. This is a model of strict complements, not an estimate of an industry. Real production may allow substitution, alternative techniques or quality changes. A planning improvement might reduce the machine time each job needs, changing a coefficient rather than simply increasing one input. The mechanism has to identify which of these claims it makes.\nWith planning capacity a, machine capacity m and one unit of each per job, the relation is:\n\ny(a,m)=\\min\\{a,m\\}.\n\nPlanning capacity a\tMachine capacity m\tCompleted-job capacity y\tExtra output from one more plan\t\n5\t10\t5\t1\t\n10\t10\t10\t0\t\n20\t10\t10\t0\t\nAt the corner a=m, adding only a plan leaves output unchanged; adding one unit of both inputs permits another job. This is why a single smooth marginal-product story needs care at the crossing.\nAfter planning reaches twenty, raising machine capacity from ten to eleven adds one possible job. The production value of that increment depends on the job's value and on whether anyone wants it. The relation locates a feasible improvement; it does not fix a supplier's price or establish a profitable investment.\nSubstitution and complementary demand\nWhere tasks are substitutes, an organisation may replace expensive work with cheaper work. Where they are complements, it may demand more of the remaining input. A hospital's scheduling, a factory's maintenance and a purely digital service need not share the same production relation. Even a highly digital activity can have a scarce complement: permission to use relevant data, a distribution channel, an accountable signatory, a customer relationship or the capacity to verify unusual cases. These are not all physical constraints. They may be contractual or institutional, and some can change when organisations redesign their work.\nAcemoglu's task-based macroeconomic analysis makes the coverage of affected tasks and the size of their cost savings central to aggregate productivity. A dramatic saving on a narrow slice does not automatically become a dramatic saving for the whole economy.\nThe constraint can move more than once\nIncreasing machine capacity may reveal a shortage of operators. Training operators may reveal weak demand. Growing demand may reveal scarce working capital. A static ranking of “the AI bottlenecks” misses this sequence. That also explains why a temporary complement need not be a durable winner. A firm can command a high price while supply is scarce and lose that margin after entry. Integration work can be essential during a transition and become routine later. A component's present importance does not establish its long-run bargaining position. The horizon changes the available alternatives. What binds during a pilot may differ from what binds at scale, after investment, entry or a redesigned process makes another input available. A scenario needs those adjustment times alongside its present capacities.\nScarcity, rent and return are three questions\nA scarce resource has a marginal value within a production problem. Its owner captures a rent only if contracts, competition and access allow that value to become income. An investor earns a return only after the price paid and the available claim on that income are considered. An essential network might be competitively priced or regulated. A supplier might spend its additional revenue expanding capacity. A customer may own the complementary asset and retain most of the saving. Saying “this input is necessary” resolves none of those cases.\nThe workshop's limiting input moves within the same production relation. If the question turns to who owns the machines and receives their income, or how separately delegated purchases fit together, that relation alone no longer answers it. The model's purpose determines which change matters: adding planning capacity calls for a new solution, while asking about income claims or coordinated decisions calls for the relationships that make those consequences intelligible.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/capital-theory/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-scarcity-value","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Where the constraint moves","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/scarcity-and-rent/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-scarcity-rent","@type":"Article","name":"Who captures value when the bottleneck moves?","headline":"Who captures value when the bottleneck moves?","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["scarcity rents","bottlenecks","control rights","outside options","switching costs","provider concentration"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/scarcity-and-rent/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Who captures value when the bottleneck moves?","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/scarcity-and-rent/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Where the constraint moves","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Who captures value when the bottleneck moves?","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/scarcity-and-rent/"}}]}]},"description":"Necessary inputs, control rights and durable rent are different economic objects.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-02-10","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Who captures value when the bottleneck moves?\nWhen one input becomes cheap, the remaining scarce inputs acquire more importance in production. Turning that importance into income requires control over access, a way to charge and a surplus after supply costs. A production constraint, a rent-bearing asset and a financial claim therefore describe different parts of the path from useful input to economic return.\nThe necessary input may have little pricing power\nImagine a digital service that needs a specialised data connection. Without it, the service cannot operate. If several suppliers offer equivalent connections and customers can switch easily, necessity need not produce large margins. Now change the arrangement: one supplier has an exclusive right to the relevant data, and switching requires a long migration. The technical role is similar, but control and outside options differ. The rent story comes from those rights and alternatives, not simply from the word “data”. A physical input can show the same distinction. Installed capacity may be scarce while providers compete for customers. An essential service may have a regulated price. A supplier may face costs or liabilities that absorb the additional revenue.\nA small cash-flow comparison makes the difference tangible. In both arrangements, the connection supports the same service. The service brings in 100, and its other operating costs are 60:\nArrangement\tConnection payment\tConnection supplier's cost\tConnection supplier's residual\tService operator's residual\t\nInterchangeable suppliers\t10\t8\t2\t30\t\nExclusive access contract\t30\t8\t22\t10\t\nThe amounts illustrate two stipulated contracts, not estimates or an explanation of how market prices settle. Total residual across the supplier and the operator remains 32. The contractual price moves twenty of it from the service operator to the connection supplier.\nA larger residual is not automatically economic rent. Some of it may compensate capital, risk or an omitted cost. Likewise, an exclusive contract does not justify any arbitrary price: customers can reduce demand, reorganise the service or find another way to meet their need. Those alternatives decide whether the arrangement survives.\nAn outside option can bound the price without selecting it. Keep the same service revenue of 100 and other costs of 60, and suppose the operator can earn a residual of 10 through an alternative arrangement. For this connection to be at least as attractive, its residual 100-60-p must be at least 10, so its maximum acceptable connection payment is 30. With supplier cost 8 and no other costs or risks, a payment below 8 cannot cover supply. The possible bargain lies between 8 and 30. The two contracts in the table occupy different points in that interval.\nNothing in that interval explains which price is agreed. Competing equivalent suppliers, the buyer's credible ability to switch, negotiation and the terms fixing future prices add the price-formation mechanism. A better outside option can lower the ceiling; an unreliable substitute may leave it high. The bottleneck supplies an opportunity to bargain, while the alternatives and contract determine how its surplus is divided.\nFollow the right, not merely the asset\nOwning a piece of equipment, operating it, holding a contract for its output and owning a company that supplies it confer different claims. A contract can reserve capacity without granting a right to all future scarcity income. A provider can control an interface while licensing the underlying asset from someone else. These distinctions matter when a capability improvement changes demand. Who can raise the price? Who bears expansion cost? Who can substitute? Who has promised a fixed service level? Which party receives the saving? The same technical bottleneck can support different incidence under different contracts. Describing an industry's “winners” without those relationships risks attaching the benefit to the most visible name rather than its recipient.\nNew supply can remove a transitional rent\nA shortage can invite entry, expansion or substitution. Higher prices may finance the capacity that later weakens those prices. A supplier that succeeds in removing a constraint can help its customers while diminishing its own scarcity advantage. There are also persistent constraints. Some locations, rights or coordination arrangements may be difficult to reproduce. Persistence still needs an explanation: high fixed costs, a legal limit, learning, network effects, customer switching costs or another mechanism. The horizon therefore belongs inside the claim. A useful bridge during a transition may not remain a toll road afterward.\nCheap capability can coexist with concentrated dependence\nA user may have access to inexpensive outputs while depending on a small set of providers. Low marginal cost and a competitive retail price do not establish a resilient supply arrangement. The Financial Stability Board's 2024 AI assessment identifies third-party dependence, provider concentration and correlated behaviour among financial-sector vulnerabilities. It does not prove that every AI market concentrates. It gives a concrete reason to examine shared infrastructure and failure channels separately from price. Open weights, portability or more providers could weaken a rent. Conversely, proprietary workflows or scarce access could preserve it even as a model's price falls. Both are countercases a serious claim has to face.\nThe duration of a rent\nA rent weakens as substitution becomes feasible, competition dissipates the surplus, customers retain more of the saving or additional capacity outruns demand. Its contractual basis matters just as much: the party operating a necessary input may lack the claim through which the surplus is paid. The production constraint helps explain why an input matters; information markets show how selection and attention can create a different scarcity from installed equipment. The durable-income question follows control, alternatives and time together. A moving bottleneck creates an opportunity to supply something useful. Whether that opportunity becomes a lasting return depends on the relationships around the supply, not just its place in the technical system.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/scarcity-and-rent/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-scarcity-value","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Where the constraint moves","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-production-income","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Who gets the gain","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Who gets the gain","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/"}}]}],"description":"How gains from useful work reach households through prices, pay, ownership and the institutions around them.","text":"A household may encounter cheaper services and less paid work at the same time. Whether it gains room to live depends on both movements, alongside its other income and commitments. The price of producing something does not alone determine the resources with which a person can buy it.\nThe connection runs through firms, contracts and institutions. Released time can support more output or a shorter working day; a saving can lower a price or increase an owner's income. A service can also help a buyer resolve a purchase that would otherwise remain uncertain. Those arrangements shape demand and investment, influencing what happens next. Following the gain means understanding how useful work enters these relationships and how they carry it to particular people.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-core-framework","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}],"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/compare-complete-execution/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-one-yardstick-docs","@type":"Article","name":"Compare complete execution","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/compare-complete-execution/"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/when-the-buyer-finds-an-answer/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-purchase-response","@type":"Article","name":"When the buyer finds an answer","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/when-the-buyer-finds-an-answer/"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/","identifier":"journey:post-agi-economic-consequence","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From capability to economic consequence","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/compare-complete-execution/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-one-yardstick-docs","@type":"Article","name":"Compare complete execution","headline":"Compare complete execution","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["human and agent execution","invoice preparation","review and correction costs","capability and adoption","household purchasing power"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/compare-complete-execution/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Compare complete execution","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/compare-complete-execution/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Who gets the gain","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Compare complete execution","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/compare-complete-execution/"}}]}]},"description":"Compare the cost of getting 100 invoices ready for review, including errors and corrections.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-09-08","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Compare complete execution\nYou want to know whether an agent can prepare invoices for review more economically than a person. Count the whole job: the result, the mistakes, the review and the cost. One Yardstick develops that comparison between people and agents. Both need a task they can attempt under the same conditions. A measurable objective, a reliable evaluator and access to the right tools make a comparison possible; they do not tell you who will perform better.\nThe same task can call for different actions\nConsider invoice preparation. The executor checks an invoice against its order and receipt, then prepares a payment proposal or sends a discrepancy for review. Give both executors the same records and access, and judge them against the same requirements.\nImportant\nThe task ends at review. Releasing funds is outside this comparison.\nWhen the records agree\nThe invoice requests payment for twelve units. The order and receipt also record twelve. Prepare a proposal with those records for the person who can approve it.\nWhen a discrepancy remains unresolved\nThe invoice and order record twelve units, but the receipt records ten. Flag the two-unit difference and include the records needed to investigate it. A faster payment proposal would not resolve the discrepancy.\nOne evaluation, applied to both\nAgree on four parts of the comparison before the trial:\nQuality: did the records warrant each proposal or escalation?\nErrors: what did a wrong action or missed exception cost?\nHuman effort: how much review, correction and escalation remained?\nTotal cost: what did execution, supporting systems and human effort require?\nA correct escalation is useful work. A quick proposal can still be wrong. The evaluator must remain reliable on the cases the executor will actually encounter, including difficult exceptions. What makes work executable? explains that distinction.\nCount the work needed to reach an accepted result\nSuppose each executor handles the same 100 invoices. After review and correction, each batch has 100 acceptable proposals or escalations. No funds have been released. These invented figures illustrate the calculation:\nWork or cost for the batch\tHuman execution\tAgent execution\t\nHuman effort performing the initial task\t240 minutes\t0 minutes\t\nHuman review\t50 minutes\t100 minutes\t\nHuman correction\t10 minutes\t20 minutes\t\nModel and tool charges\tUSD 10\tUSD 20\t\nAccepted results after review and correction\t100\t100\t\nAt USD 30 per hour of human effort, the human arrangement costs USD 160: five hours plus USD 10 in tool charges. The agent arrangement costs USD 80: two hours plus USD 20 in model and tool charges. That is USD 1.60 and USD 0.80 per accepted result. The zero in the first row means no human performed the agent's initial step; its machine cost is included in the charges.\nThe cheaper arrangement still required twice as much review and correction. Keep each initial result beside its reviewed outcome so that the final count does not hide missed discrepancies, unnecessary escalations or correct requests for help. A serious error that breaches a requirement remains a failure even when average cost is low.\nNote\nThe example's cost assumptions. This example assumes no additional setup or operating expense. A real comparison needs those costs, the intended mix of invoices and an agreed review procedure. Different cases, prices or review effort can reverse the result; the arithmetic is not evidence of either executor's performance.\nAdoption is a different event\nA successful trial does not show that organisations will use an executor repeatedly. To study adoption, define the population and what counts as recurring use at an acceptable quality and cost. Then observe that event.\nOne Yardstick proposes a transition clock for the economy. Testing that ambition also requires examining particular frontiers: a capability becoming accessible, a deployment becoming reliable or an organisation changing its work. These processes need not move at the same pace. A forecast needs an observable process and an outcome defined before it is known.\nThe buyers remain in the picture\nCheaper execution does not settle who can buy the output. An agent does not need the income with which a displaced worker bought a service. Follow the production gain into prices, wages, ownership and public arrangements to understand how it changes a household's available resources.\nThe broader Post-AGI Economics inquiry studies those frontiers, moving constraints and income arrangements alongside the clock. Comparing one task contributes evidence about execution. Linking it to an economic transition requires an account of adoption, the work around the tool and where the income goes.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/compare-complete-execution/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"about":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/#entity","identifier":"project:one-yardstick","@type":"CreativeWork","name":"One Yardstick","subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/","identifier":"content:post-agi-one-yardstick","@type":"WebPage","name":"One Yardstick","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/mentions/","identifier":"content:one-yardstick-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"One Yardstick mentions","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/mentions/"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/#entity","identifier":"project:post-agi-economics","@type":"ResearchProject","name":"Post-AGI Economics","subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/","identifier":"content:post-agi-economics-home","@type":"WebPage","name":"When intelligence gets cheap","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-production-income","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Who gets the gain","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/","identifier":"journey:post-agi-economic-consequence","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From capability to economic consequence","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Who gets the gain","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"From capability to economic consequence","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/"}}]}],"description":"Follow a capability through deployed work, production and labour to what reaches households.","text":"An attractive result from one task can tempt us to jump straight to the future of work. The intervening questions determine whether the result travels: can it become dependable work in an organisation, what response does that work produce, and whose income or spending changes?\nThe order begins with an execution comparison so that the supposed gain has a definite meaning. It then follows the surrounding organisation before examining labour, household resources and institutional arrangements. A later conclusion can depend on an earlier link being established, but it cannot be read off it. That is why a purchase response can be a real benefit while its labour and distributional consequences remain worth investigating.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-production-income","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Who gets the gain","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/"}],"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":5,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/evaluation-and-execution/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-evaluation-execution","@type":"Article","name":"What makes work executable?","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/evaluation-and-execution/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/organisational-complements/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-organisational-complements","@type":"Article","name":"The work around the tool","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/organisational-complements/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/labor-economics/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-labour","@type":"Article","name":"The changing work behind an occupation","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/labor-economics/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/productivity-and-purchasing-power/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-buyer-capacity","@type":"Article","name":"Who gets to buy what machines produce?","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/productivity-and-purchasing-power/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":5,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/institutions-and-distribution/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-institutional-closure","@type":"Article","name":"Same technology, different economy","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/institutions-and-distribution/"}}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/evaluation-and-execution/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-evaluation-execution","@type":"Article","name":"What makes work executable?","headline":"What makes work executable?","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["Agent Execution Hypothesis","workflow evaluation","invoice automation","full operating cost","human assistance"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/evaluation-and-execution/","@type":"WebPage","name":"What makes work executable?","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/evaluation-and-execution/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":4,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Who gets the gain","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"From capability to economic consequence","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"name":"What makes work executable?","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/evaluation-and-execution/"}}]}]},"description":"An execution hypothesis connects measurable work to automation, its full cost and the buyers who depend on the income it replaces.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-11-04","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"What makes work executable?\nWhen a job has a measurable objective, a reliable way to judge the result and tools that can act, what still requires its executor to be human? The Agent Execution Hypothesis proposes that an artificial agent can, in principle, match or exceed human execution under the same inputs, tools and constraints. It is a claim to test, not a result obtained merely by specifying the task.\nThe economic stakes follow from that claim. If an agent can deliver the required quality at a lower complete cost, a firm has a reason to substitute it for some human work. If checking, integration or handling exceptions consumes the saving, cheaper model output need not make the job cheaper. And if substitution removes a worker’s income, the agent does not become the customer that worker was. Execution, its complementary work and the buyer belong in the same inquiry.\nA signal does not choose its recipient\nChoosing what to optimise is different from executing well against that objective. Hayek’s account of prices explains how people can coordinate using knowledge dispersed among them, without each knowing every circumstance behind a price. The extension in One Yardstick, One Clock is that a person or a machine could respond to a useful signal if it can interpret it and has the means to act. The signal’s usefulness does not by itself establish either executor’s competence.\nAn evaluator makes that proposal concrete for a task. It must distinguish the outcome we value from a convenient proxy. Tacit skill does not disappear because we cannot easily score it: if its contribution cannot be captured well enough for the comparison, the execution claim has not yet been made testable there.\nFollow the objective into an invoice\nPay the right supplier, the right amount, at the right time, without duplicates. The objective fits in one sentence. Following it into an invoice workflow reveals a much larger task: an ambiguous supplier, a changed bank instruction, conflicting documents and an approval still needed before money moves.\nGive both executors the same process to carry through, including the moments when they need help. The evaluator can then judge what they did and what they handed over for review.\nImportant\nThe comparison ends before money moves. The task is to prepare a payment proposal or collect the records needed to review an unresolved discrepancy.\nPart of the comparison\tIllustrative specification\t\nCases\tRoutine invoices, duplicate submissions, disputed quantities and changed bank instructions; report each category separately\t\nInformation\tThe same invoice, purchase order, receipt and dated supplier record for both executors\t\nAllowed actions\tRead those records, prepare a proposal, or escalate an unresolved discrepancy\t\nAcceptance\tSupplier, amount and supporting records agree; every unresolved instruction change is escalated\t\nConsequential error\tA proposal treated as ready despite an unresolved bank instruction, duplicate or unsupported amount\t\nWhole cost\tPreparation and review time, tool use, retries and the work required to resolve escalations\t\nA record saying “supplier instruction changed” can justify asking for confirmation without identifying which instruction is genuine. Correct escalation is then a useful result. A resolved test case and an unresolved live case must not be scored as if they supplied the same information.\nThe objective meets the documents\nIn this hypothetical workflow, a test can supply answers that the real process has to discover. If an evaluator learns which bank instruction was valid only after a payment, its feedback can classify the loss but cannot prevent it. Delayed consequences, partial information and a shifting case mix therefore belong inside the test. A system that handles ordinary invoices well may still need a different procedure for a rare, consequential exception. Abstention and escalation can be part of competent execution. The comparison should account for the time and work they require, rather than score every request for help as a failure or treat that help as free.\nThe same task under the same conditions\nAccess changes what an executor can do. A human able to call a colleague has a different means of resolving an ambiguity from an agent limited to the supplied documents. An agent with additional records may perform better without having demonstrated superiority under identical inputs. A fair comparison specifies the task distribution, information, tools, time budget and permitted escalation. It also specifies which errors matter. Average accuracy can obscure a rare mistake whose loss dominates the value of many correctly handled cases.\nThe configuration is part of the comparison. Recommending a payment, preparing it and releasing it involve different effects and responsibilities. An assistant can create value by improving preparation while a person retains approval. Autonomous execution requires its own performance and authority conditions.\nFrom a cheap draft to completed work\nThe full cost includes checking, retries, monitoring, integration, incident handling, human escalation and delay. Some costs are fixed and spread over more cases; others grow with complexity or consequence. The baseline is the existing process with its actual overhead and errors, not an imaginary flawless human. If preparation becomes faster but review takes longer, the net saving may be small. If routine work becomes easier and people can handle exceptions better, the gain may come through a changed allocation of work rather than replacement. What matters is accepted work at the required quality and risk. Compare complete execution works through the preparation, review and correction costs for a hundred invoices.\nAuthorisation determines which of those effects an actor may attempt. It is separate from measured performance. The NIST AI Risk Management Framework addresses risk across development and use, making it a useful reference for this deployment boundary rather than a certification of any particular executor.\nAn execution claim with something to compare\nA local claim can say that, for this workflow and case distribution, a configuration reaches the required outcome at a stated cost and risk. Matched performance and deployment evidence can then support or contradict it. A universal claim about all instrumentable workflows would need substantially more: a successful example cannot establish every case, and an imagined better-trained executor cannot indefinitely stand in for a specified one.\nThe yardstick earns its place by showing where capability works, where assistance is preferable and where the process still needs another means of acting. The organisation around the tool then determines how that local performance becomes productive use.\nFollow the saving one step further. Does it lower the customer’s price, increase someone’s income or accrue to the owner of a scarce complement? A person doing less invoice work might take on other work, share in the gain or lose income. Those are different paths to household purchasing power. The invoice comparison can test execution and its cost. Understanding the economy it changes requires following both the work that remains and the income that reaches its buyers.\nF. A. Hayek, “The Use of Knowledge in Society”, American Economic Review 35(4), September 1945, pp. 519–530. The proposed extension to artificial executors belongs to the Agent Execution Hypothesis.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/evaluation-and-execution/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/","identifier":"journey:post-agi-economic-consequence","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From capability to economic consequence","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/institutions-and-distribution/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-institutional-closure","@type":"Article","name":"Same technology, different economy","headline":"Same technology, different economy","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["model closure","ownership","distribution of gains","price pass-through","worker ownership","public provision"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/institutions-and-distribution/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Same technology, different economy","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/institutions-and-distribution/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":4,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Who gets the gain","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"From capability to economic consequence","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"name":"Same technology, different economy","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/institutions-and-distribution/"}}]}]},"description":"One cost saving can reach customers, a supplier, worker-owners or a public budget. Ownership and the rules around it decide which.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-05-05","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Same technology, different economy\nTwo economies can have the same technical capacity and distribute its benefits differently. If we want to understand welfare or buyer capacity, ownership and institutions belong inside the argument, not in a paragraph added after a production forecast. Economists call part of this specification a closure: the rules that say what adjusts, what is held fixed, how markets clear and who receives residual income. A closure is necessary to solve a model. It is not necessarily a description of the only possible world.\nOne production gain, several arrangements\nTake a hypothetical service that costs 100 to produce before a new tool and 70 afterward, for unchanged quality and quantity. The saving of 30 gives us a common production change against which to compare different arrangements. Under competitive pass-through, the service's price could fall. Under a protected contract, the supplier could retain much of the saving. Under worker ownership, a residual might reach the workers through their ownership claim rather than their hourly wage. Under public provision, a budget saving might support other services.\nA supplier may use the saving to expand, improve quality, service debt or compensate risk. A public budget saving is not automatically paid to households. A lower price helps consumers in proportion to their use, not necessarily in proportion to need. The same cost reduction therefore cannot identify the recipient without describing the surrounding arrangement.\nKeep the immediate claim separate from what its recipient later chooses to do:\nArrangement\tFirst claim on the benefit\tNext decision affecting the economy\t\nCompetitive pass-through\tCustomers through lower prices\tBuy more of this service, spend elsewhere or save\t\nSupplier retains the difference\tSupplier's residual account\tInvest, distribute, repay an obligation or retain cash\t\nWorker-held residual\tWorkers in their capacity as owners\tAllocate between distribution and the enterprise's needs\t\nPublic provision\tThe relevant public budget\tChange the service, taxation or another expenditure through its budget process\t\nThe rows do not promise any behavioural response. Nor are they mutually exclusive labels for whole economies: a supplier can have worker owners and face strong competition. The comparison specifies a channel and then asks how it operates.\nDistribution changes the next production decision\nThe first-round allocation can affect demand, investment and bargaining. A worker receiving more income may consume differently from an asset owner receiving the same amount. A firm retaining the saving may expand capacity, hold cash or return it to owners. Their choices depend on expectations and constraints, not only accounting. That feedback makes a static allocation incomplete for some purposes. If the question is immediate incidence, a short-run comparison may suffice. If it is the path of demand and capacity, behaviour and adjustment over time matter. A model that originally held ownership and demand fixed can still answer its narrow question. It becomes inadequate when we ask it to compare institutions it never represented. Comparing those institutions requires a change in the model's question or closure.\nGovernance chooses more than a score\nMaximising output, protecting a consumption standard, reducing inequality, preserving meaningful participation and limiting environmental harm are different objectives. A productivity evaluator cannot decide how to trade them off. It can sometimes show the consequences of a chosen rule. It cannot make the rule legitimate simply because it can compute an optimum. A constrained optimisation also inherits the omissions of its objective and constraints. Unmeasured harms do not become acceptable by disappearing from the data. The separation between deciding and executing is thus substantive. Who may change the objective, who may act, who bears losses and who can challenge the decision are institutional questions.\nDifferent arrangements, different paths\nA highly automated economy can support strong household demand or concentrate gains while buyers lose income. Public demand, exports, shared ownership, new goods, price adjustment and non-market provision introduce further possibilities. Those channels make buyer capacity a question about the economy's arrangements, not a binary destination selected by its technical capacity. A useful comparison states the resource constraint, funding mechanism, incidence, behavioural response and feasibility of each alternative. It also tests whether the effect survives a simpler explanation such as ordinary competition or a temporary investment cycle.\nThe household consequences depend on these ownership and adjustment conditions, and on the price arrangement that Where does the saving go? follows. The technology expands a feasible set. Institutions determine who can act within it, receive its gains and challenge its costs.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/institutions-and-distribution/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/","identifier":"journey:post-agi-economic-consequence","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From capability to economic consequence","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/labor-economics/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-labour","@type":"Article","name":"The changing work behind an occupation","headline":"The changing work behind an occupation","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["AI task exposure","firm adoption","worker productivity","early-career hiring","employment evidence"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/labor-economics/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The changing work behind an occupation","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/labor-economics/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":4,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Who gets the gain","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"From capability to economic consequence","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"name":"The changing work behind an occupation","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/labor-economics/"}}]}]},"description":"Task exposure, workplace assistance and early-career hiring reveal different parts of how AI reaches workers.","datePublished":{"@value":"2026-11-04","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The changing work behind an occupation\nTwo accounting teams do similar work. One has clean records, stable interfaces and a manager able to redesign approvals. The other spends its days reconciling inconsistent documents and finding missing authorisations. An occupation-level exposure score can place them together while the technology reaches their work very differently.\nThe occupational label hides the work of those two hypothetical teams. Capability reaches tasks through an organisation; changed tasks can alter output, hours, hiring and pay. The evidence becomes much more interesting when we keep those objects in view. An exposure score, a resolved support issue and an entry-level hire each reveal a different part of the movement.\nA task description is not a deployment record\nExposure measures compare descriptions of work with an assessment of what a technology could do. That can locate an opportunity or a vulnerability. It does not observe a firm's permissions, data quality, integration costs or willingness to change its process. The two teams' different capacity to reorganise makes this gap substantive, rather than merely a delay before an identical deployment reaches both.\nActual adoption also means several things. A person drafting emails, a team using suggestions in every case, and a system closing cases without human intervention are not interchangeable treatments. Pulito and coauthors' Danish study links surveys to employer-worker records and finds adoption associated with firm capacity, digital infrastructure and workforce composition; exposure measures differ in how well they predict it. That is evidence about who adopts, not an estimate of what adoption does to wages.\nProductivity can rise through different routes\nA support assistant may help novices recognise a problem more quickly. A planning system may reduce avoidable rework. Automation may remove a task, while new verification work takes its place. Each can raise output per worker through a different mechanism. Brynjolfsson, Li and Raymond's May 2025 journal article follows 5,172 customer-support agents at one business-software firm. Its staggered introduction of assistance raises issues resolved per hour by about 15%, with unequal gains across experience levels. It is useful precisely because the work and treatment are specific. It does not estimate an economy-wide employment response.\nAldasoro and coauthors' January 2026 European firm study instead matches survey and financial data and uses US peer adoption as an instrument. It estimates higher labour productivity, consistent with capital deepening, without short-run employment losses; complementary investment and firm size matter. The causal interpretation depends on that instrument's assumptions. Neither the short horizon nor the sampled firms establishes that displacement cannot happen elsewhere or later.\nIf demand expands, saved time may support more output. If demand is fixed, the same saving may reduce hours or headcount. If the firm improves quality, a quantity measure can miss part of the gain. A task-level result does not select its destination.\nA deployment can also change demand for the work. A buyer finding the answer needed to purchase creates a different possibility from completing an existing support case faster. The labour response still depends on the resulting work, its cost and the firm’s decisions.\nLittle average change can coexist with changing tasks\nHumlum and Vestergaard's 13 March 2026 study links surveys in eleven exposed Danish occupations to administrative records through December 2024. In its adopter comparison, differential earnings and recorded-hours effects are near zero, with effects larger than about 2% ruled out over the two years after ChatGPT's launch. Yet tasks change and new oversight work appears. A small average difference between adopters and non-adopters is compatible with substantial reorganisation. A change common to both groups is not identified directly by that comparison.\nExpectations are another observation. The March 2026 revision of Yotzov and coauthors' firm survey covers almost 6,000 executives in the US, UK, Germany and Australia. Most report little employment or productivity impact over the preceding three years, while expecting larger changes over the next three. These are reports and forecasts, not a randomised estimate of adoption's effect. Its October 2026 revision adds countries and a later survey and is a separate observation.\nEarly-career harm deserves a specific explanation\nLee Tucker's April 2026 analysis finds a marked decline in early-career hiring in highly exposed industry-state cells after ChatGPT. Its broader comparisons also reveal pandemic-era trend changes. A historical decomposition suggests monetary-policy shocks through 2023 could account for up to one quarter of the relative early-career employment decline through the second quarter of 2025. The analysis finds no evidence that those shocks explain the rapid relative decline in hires. That distinction matters when asking what caused the fall in hiring. Exposure is not observed firm deployment, so the study does not directly trace the whole automation mechanism.\nAudoly, Guerin and Topa's May 2026 job-posting analysis finds that exposed occupations' relative weakness predates ChatGPT and no clear additional junior-senior divergence. Postings are not completed hires. The studies differ in outcomes, groupings and comparisons; treating one as settling the other would discard the question each can answer.\nA firm might reduce junior hiring before deploying a tool because it expects future substitution. It might also reduce hiring because demand weakened. Separating those explanations requires expectations and deployment observations alongside the same firms' hiring, separations and task changes.\nA change in the question can change the adoption rate\nThe April 2026 Bonney and coauthors study separates firm adoption, business-function deployment and worker-task use in the US Census Business Trends and Outlook Survey. It reports 18% of firms using AI in at least one function during November 2025–January 2026, or 32% when weighted by employment. Those are different denominators. Neither is the fraction of all work performed autonomously by AI. Changing survey wording also interrupts a simple comparison with the earlier use series; a jump in the reported number can reflect the instrument as well as the economy.\nHighfill and Samuels' July 2026 BEA study adds another gap: stated intentions do not straightforwardly predict realised changes in production. It compares expected with actual adoption and separately connects earlier adoption motives to industry accounts. The industry account and a worker's task record can both be useful while answering different questions. Joining them requires a defensible relationship between the populations, periods and measures.\nFollowing the gain to the worker\nThe support study can reveal a mechanism by which assistance helps a novice. The hiring study can reveal a troubling change without observing that mechanism. Connecting them needs evidence on deployment and the same workers or firms, not just a shared date or the word “AI”.\nA convincing account connects actual use to quality-adjusted output, full operating cost, prices, hours, employment and pay. It then asks whether the effects persist, spill over to competitors, or shift expenditure between products rather than expand it. The result may be augmentation in one setting and displacement in another. That heterogeneity is the finding to explain, not noise to average out. Even established production gains leave the household's ability to buy the output to be explained. Lower prices, changed hours, wages and income claims can move its purchasing power in different directions; the worker's place in production is one part of that larger account.\nBrynjolfsson, Li and Raymond, Generative AI at Work, Quarterly Journal of Economics 140(2), May 2025. This is the journal edition; earlier working-paper versions report different figures.\nHumlum and Vestergaard, Still Waters, Rapid Currents, RFBerlin DP 078/26, 13 March 2026. The bound concerns relative changes for the stated comparison and period, not every effect of AI on the economy.\nYotzov et al., Firm Data on AI, March 2026 revision, first issued in February 2026. The paper's edition page also lists later versions. Survey responses are not causal treatment effects, and expected changes are not realised changes.\nBonney et al., The Microstructure of AI Diffusion, April 2026 NBER working paper. Firm, function and task use are separate observations. The October Census revision is a distinct edition.\nHighfill and Samuels, AI Expectations and Outcomes, BEA working paper, July 2026. The adoption comparison uses 2023–2026 survey observations; the motives-to-outcomes exercise uses earlier business-survey motives and industry production accounts.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/labor-economics/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/","identifier":"journey:post-agi-economic-consequence","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From capability to economic consequence","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/organisational-complements/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-organisational-complements","@type":"Article","name":"The work around the tool","headline":"The work around the tool","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["organisational complements","Productivity J-Curve","process redesign","intangible investment","adoption selection"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/organisational-complements/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The work around the tool","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/organisational-complements/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":4,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Who gets the gain","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"From capability to economic consequence","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"name":"The work around the tool","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/organisational-complements/"}}]}]},"description":"Co-investment, process redesign and learning determine how a local capability becomes production.","datePublished":{"@value":"2026-04-13","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The work around the tool\nA team reduces report drafting from two days to two hours. The report still waits a week for reconciliation and approval. The impressive component improvement has barely changed when anybody receives the work.\nThat hypothetical queue makes the organisation around a tool visible. Better source data, a different handoff or clear responsibility for an exception can make faster drafting useful. Calling all of it \"adoption lag\" hides the work of changing the process. The return depends on which of those investments other tasks can reuse and how long it takes for the savings to cover their cost.\nThe process can defeat the component\nIf the faster draft creates more discrepancies for reviewers, total work may even grow. The saving changes one stage while reconciliation or approval still limits the system. Removing that limit may require better source data, fewer handoffs, a revised approval rule or an accountable owner for exceptions. Managerial attention, training time and trust become inputs to the improvement, alongside the software. Multiplying the drafting saving by the number of workers would miss both those costs and the remaining queue.\nPut the illustrative stages on the same clock, using an eight-hour working day:\nStage\tEarlier process\tFaster drafting only\tDrafting plus redesigned handoff\t\nGather and reconcile inputs\t8 hours\t8 hours\t4 hours\t\nDraft\t16 hours\t2 hours\t2 hours\t\nWait for review slot\t40 hours\t40 hours\t8 hours\t\nReview and acceptance\t4 hours\t6 hours\t4 hours\t\nElapsed working time\t68 hours\t56 hours\t18 hours\t\nActive work across these stages\t28 hours\t16 hours\t10 hours\t\nThese are designed process times, not observations. The middle column assumes a faster draft requires two extra review hours. The last assumes input quality and the review handoff also improve. It does not credit all fifty saved elapsed hours to the drafting tool. Review-slot capacity and reconciliation have changed too.\nElapsed time follows when a recipient can use the report. Active work follows labour consumed by these stages. A shorter queue can matter greatly to the recipient without saving an equal number of worker-hours. Conversely, released labour may not increase output while demand or another stage remains fixed.\nComplementary investment changes what we observe\nBrynjolfsson, Rock and Syverson's Productivity J-Curve examines how general-purpose technologies require complementary intangible investment, including new processes, skills and business models. If some of that investment is poorly measured, the timing of measured productivity can differ from the timing of underlying economic work. A delayed payoff and a failed project can look similar early on. Distinguishing them requires observing the complementary work as well as the resulting process: whether data improves, handoffs change and accepted work becomes faster or less costly. Spending on software and training describes the investment; the changed outcome describes its return.\nReadiness also selects who adopts\nA capable firm may adopt first because it already has useful data, skilled staff and the ability to redesign a process. Comparing adopters with non-adopters can then mix the effect of the tool with the effect of that pre-existing capacity. The Danish adoption study by Pulito and coauthors finds strong associations with firm capacity and workforce readiness. The result motivates this selection concern; it does not prove that every adopter's productivity gain is caused by those characteristics.\nA credible test may use a rollout, a suitable instrument or a comparison with defensible pre-trends. It also has to ask what the treatment contains. A “tool rollout” accompanied by process redesign estimates a package, not necessarily the isolated model.\nScaling can reveal different complements\nA small team can resolve exceptions informally. A large deployment may need consistent permissions, monitoring, escalation and incident response. The organisation that works for a pilot may not work for scale. Some complements are reusable: a sound data interface can support several tools. Others are highly specific and can become stranded if the system changes. Treating all integration spending as durable capital misses that difference. The relevant account includes useful work after review, total cost, reliability, learning and the ability to replace a supplier. It also asks whether benefits spread across the organisation or concentrate in teams that already had the best conditions.\nComplements and their payoff\nThe complement hypothesis becomes specific when improved data or coordination changes the measured result under a credible comparison. It weakens when checking consumes the saving, redesigned processes yield no material gain, or demand does not value the additional output. The production relation locates the limiting input, while changes in workers' hours, employment and pay reveal how the resulting reorganisation reaches them. The same process improvement can support more output or fewer hours, depending on demand and how the work is arranged.\nThe organisation is doing more than installing a component. It is deciding how the component fits into work, what other work becomes necessary and whether the combined process earns its cost. That is where a local capability becomes an economic change.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/organisational-complements/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/","identifier":"journey:post-agi-economic-consequence","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From capability to economic consequence","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/productivity-and-purchasing-power/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-buyer-capacity","@type":"Article","name":"Who gets to buy what machines produce?","headline":"Who gets to buy what machines produce?","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["household purchasing power","automation gains","income channels","consumer demand","income floors","household budgets"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/productivity-and-purchasing-power/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Who gets to buy what machines produce?","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/productivity-and-purchasing-power/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":4,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Who gets the gain","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"From capability to economic consequence","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"name":"Who gets to buy what machines produce?","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/productivity-and-purchasing-power/"}}]}]},"description":"Cheaper services can coexist with a household unable to afford them. Follow prices, lost wages and other income through its budget.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-04-21","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Who gets to buy what machines produce?\nIf machines do more of the work, people still need a way to buy what they produce. That is the buyer question behind Post-AGI Economics. Lower prices can help, and so can new work, ownership income or transfers. But they reach different people, under different conditions. A production gain can become a cheaper purchase, greater earnings or a return someone else receives. Follow it into the household's budget to find out which.\nLower prices can help without offsetting every lost income\nConsider a hypothetical household that receives 100 units of income, spends 60 on housing and other commitments, and has 40 left for a basket of services. If that basket becomes 20 percent cheaper, the original basket costs 32. The household gains room in its budget. Now suppose its income falls to 80 while those commitments stay at 60. It has only 20 available. The price reduction does not restore the basket. The average productivity gain leaves out the position of someone who loses income while a large part of their expenses does not fall.\nHousehold budget\tBefore\tCheaper services\tCheaper services and lower income\t\nIncome\t100\t100\t80\t\nFixed commitments\t60\t60\t60\t\nAvailable for the service basket\t40\t40\t20\t\nCost of the original basket\t40\t32\t32\t\nBalance after buying that basket\t0\t8\t−12\t\nFor this fixed basket and fixed commitments, an income loss of eight would just exhaust the price saving. A loss of twenty exceeds it. In symbols, with income Y, commitments F and basket cost B:\n\n\\text{room after purchase}=Y-F-B.\n\nThe illustration does not say prices or obligations remain fixed in an actual transition. It makes those assumptions visible. Another household may own an asset whose income rises or receive a transfer; the identical service-price reduction then meets a different budget.\nThe distribution can disappear even when an aggregate account balances. Extend the example to two identical households, each initially earning 100 with commitments of 60 and a basket costing 40. After the change, let the first earn 80 and the second 120, with both baskets now costing 32. Total income remains 200; buying both baskets now costs 64 rather than 80. The aggregate has 16 units to spare after commitments and those purchases. Yet the first household is short by 12 while the second has 28 left. That surplus does not reach the first household merely because it exists in the total. A transfer, shared ownership or another income channel would be an additional arrangement, with its own conditions.\nLabour income is one channel, not the whole budget\nA technology can affect hours, wages, employment, entry into occupations and the timing of payments. Household resources may also include profits, dividends, rent, transfers or earnings from other members. Saving and credit can smooth some shocks, but smoothing is different from replacing a lasting income stream. The household is therefore not interchangeable with a worker. A worker-level wage observation omits other members and assets. Aggregate labour income omits its distribution. A growing output total omits both.\nNor is consumer demand the only source of demand. Investment, public spending and exports can support production. New goods may create demand that a fixed product basket misses. These are possible offsets that an argument about weak buyers must examine, not assume away.\nAn income floor has a level and a denominator\nSuppose households consume 80 while total output is 100. If household consumption stays at 80 and output rises to 120, consumption's share falls from 80 percent to about 67 percent. That falling share alone does not establish that households became poorer. The reverse trap is to protect a share without specifying the level, prices or distribution. An unchanged aggregate share can coexist with losses concentrated among particular households.\nAn income floor must therefore say what it protects: nominal resources, a purchasing-power standard, essential consumption, or a share of income or output. These are different objectives. It must also say how it is funded and how prices, taxes, output and recipients respond. A formula that drops capital income or treats output as fixed has chosen its assumptions, not solved every possible economy. The buyer-rich and buyer-thin outcomes in One Yardstick, One Clock describe different distributional possibilities under those conditions. Comparing them requires a stated consumption standard, income channels and price response, rather than treating a change in output share as a conclusion about household welfare.\nDemand can amplify a shock, but the chain needs evidence\nIf households facing an income loss reduce spending, local firms may lose revenue and hire less. Debt obligations or costly housing can make adjustment harder. But the size and duration depend on which households lose income, what they stop buying, which prices adjust and what other demand replaces theirs. A small saving on digital services may matter little next to housing. A production gain in an exporting firm may reach domestic households through wages or taxes rather than local sales. A capital-intensive provider may reinvest gains for years before distributing them. The relevant time horizon is part of each mechanism.\nThe strongest evidence would connect deployment and production to the same workers and households, their full resources, prices and expenditure. Task and firm studies observe particular parts of that chain: assistance, output, hiring or wages. Following the household requires the links among those observations as well as resources beyond one job.\nTransfers, ownership and tax are different answers\nConcern about buyer capacity does not by itself select a transfer, ownership arrangement or tax rule. Those alternatives differ in administrative feasibility, incentives, funding, risk and who receives the benefit. They also embody choices about what the economy is supposed to achieve. Different institutional arrangements can give the same production improvement different paths into household resources. Output gives the economy something to distribute; income, obligations and prices determine what a household can do with its share.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/productivity-and-purchasing-power/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/","identifier":"journey:post-agi-economic-consequence","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From capability to economic consequence","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/when-the-buyer-finds-an-answer/#article","identifier":"content:post-agi-purchase-response","@type":"Article","name":"When the buyer finds an answer","headline":"When the buyer finds an answer","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"keywords":["generative AI retail experiments","purchase conversion","randomised field experiments","search friction","return on deployment","demand substitution"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/when-the-buyer-finds-an-answer/","@type":"WebPage","name":"When the buyer finds an answer","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/when-the-buyer-finds-an-answer/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"What breaks when thinking gets cheap","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Who gets the gain","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"When the buyer finds an answer","item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/when-the-buyer-finds-an-answer/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/productivity-and-purchasing-power/","https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/labor-economics/","https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/organisational-complements/"]},"description":"A deployed service can change whether a transaction happens; its sales effect, full return and wider demand consequences are different questions.","datePublished":{"@value":"2026-07-06","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"When the buyer finds an answer\nA buyer hesitates because a product description leaves a question unresolved. An answer can change whether the purchase happens. In that familiar situation, the useful service is more than a cheaper way to process a transaction already certain to occur. It changes the possibility of the transaction itself.\nThis gives AI deployment an economic path different from saving minutes on an unchanged task. The distinction matters because sales, operating cost and the work needed to fulfil an order can move together. We need to follow that movement, rather than select a labour-saving story before observing what changed.\nSeven retail experiments on whether buyers purchase more\nThe demand side need not remain a hypothetical destination for a production gain. Fang and coauthors' June 2026 paper examines seven randomised GenAI workflow experiments on one cross-border retail platform in 2023–24. They span service, matching, advertising and seller assistance. Sales effects range from no detectable effect to 16.3 percent against the workflow's existing practice. The gains operate mainly through more people buying, rather than bigger carts; returns and ratings do not deteriorate in the reported comparisons.\nAnnualising the four positive sales effects gives an estimated USD 4.6–5.2 in additional sales per consumer, assuming linear addition without workflow overlap or synergies. It is a calculation from short experimental periods, not a year of observed extra sales. The study does not observe the full capital, engineering, inference and organisational costs, so the sales estimate is not net return. These details leave a constructive result intact: particular deployed workflows changed purchases in a randomised comparison.\nA transaction becomes possible\nThe mechanism has a different economic shape from an assistant completing the same support case faster. If a buyer finds a suitable item or gets an answer needed to decide, work can improve by resolving a transaction that otherwise would not happen. Demand and the useful service change together. The firm's next question is whether the extra transactions repay the deployment and change its need for support, fulfilment or other work. A labour-saving story is only one possible account.\nThe experiments do not isolate that explanation from persuasion, attention, trust or novelty. More purchases could follow clearer information, more compelling presentation or some combination of them. The difference matters to the product: a buyer finding a better match and a buyer being persuaded to act can produce the same immediate sales result while leaving different reasons to return. Conversion locates the observed change; understanding the service requires following the buyer beyond it.\nThe retailer and the economy\nFor the retailer, resolving that hesitation can create an additional sale. For the buyer, it might replace a purchase elsewhere or bring a future purchase forward. Lower search friction can improve the choice without increasing the buyer's total budget. Following the gain beyond the platform requires those alternatives to be observed, together with the additional work, hours, pay and income claims that the transactions create. The experiment establishes a local purchase response; tracing its wider destination is a further economic question.\nA local purchase response is worth understanding in its own right. It can reveal an unresolved need that better information helps a buyer meet. Whether the response creates a lasting return depends on the complete operating process, including what additional transactions require. Whether it changes the buyer's room to live depends on income and prices beyond that purchase. Finding an answer can improve a transaction without answering every question about its economic destination.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/when-the-buyer-finds-an-answer/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-production-income","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Who gets the gain","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/","identifier":"content:post-agi-one-yardstick","@type":"WebPage","name":"One Yardstick","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"description":"A living thesis about comparable execution, the time available to adapt and who can buy what agents produce.","text":"One Yardstick\n“Agents don't buy jeans.” Better execution raises a question beyond what a machine can do: who can buy the result? One Yardstick connects a common measure of human and agent execution to the time available for an economy to adapt, and to the way its gains reach households.\nOne Yardstick, One Clock develops the full argument, from a common test of execution to the buyer question. It is a living thesis. The proposed transition clock and the inquiry into moving constraints and rents develop alongside each other. The clock has no empirical forecast yet. A worked invoice comparison gives the first part of the argument something concrete to measure.\nFrom useful execution to economic change\nA better answer is only the beginning. Useful work needs a meaningful evaluation, the means to act and an operating cost someone can sustain. Repeated use can then change the organisation of work, the value of scarce inputs and who receives the gains.\nFollow the work into the economy\nA trial can tell you whether an agent met the requirements for one job. It takes different observations to tell whether firms will use it every week and whether households benefit when they do.\nDefine useful work\nChoose a task and the result that matters. Include ordinary cases and exceptions, the information and tools available, and who decides whether the result is acceptable.\nCompare complete execution\nEvaluate human and agent execution on common conditions. Count consequential mistakes, correction and review effort, and the resources needed to complete the work.\nFollow recurring use\nExamine what makes the capability economical to adopt, which constraints move and how the gain reaches households through prices, wages, ownership or public arrangements.\nQuestions about One Yardstick\nWhat does the yardstick measure?\nExecution of a defined task under common conditions: the usefulness of the result, consequential mistakes, human effort and complete operating cost. The evaluator must remain meaningful for the cases encountered. Defining a yardstick makes a capability test possible; it does not establish in advance who will perform better.\nCan an escalation count as useful work?\nYes, when the records warrant it. Preparing a proposal and escalating an unresolved discrepancy are judged under the same task, including accepted work, consequential errors, review effort and complete operating cost.\nWhy does the framework follow household income?\nMore productive execution changes who is paid and what becomes cheaper. Households may receive the gains through lower prices, labour income, ownership or public arrangements. Following those channels connects the technical comparison to the economy in which the resulting goods and services must be used.\nHow do capability and adoption differ?\nCapability concerns whether the work can be done under the stated conditions. Adoption also depends on dependable recurring use, integration, switching costs and whether the complete arrangement is economical. Comparing execution makes those questions more precise; adoption needs its own evidence.\nWhat is the transition clock for?\nThe clock asks how the surrounding economy changes, how long people have to adapt and which economy that change leads to, where the yardstick compares execution on one task. It proposes following economic regimes towards agent-dominant execution, distinguishing an economy whose households retain purchasing power from one whose buyers lose it. The time between frontiers explains what such a clock needs, and A forecast meets its outcome shows how a dated forecast is scored against what happened. Beside the clock, the wider Post-AGI Economics inquiry asks where constraints and rents move and how the arrangements around production change.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"about":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/#entity","identifier":"project:one-yardstick","@type":"CreativeWork","name":"One Yardstick","subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/","identifier":"content:post-agi-one-yardstick","@type":"WebPage","name":"One Yardstick","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/mentions/","identifier":"content:one-yardstick-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"One Yardstick mentions","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/mentions/"}]}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/#entity","identifier":"project:post-agi-economics","@type":"ResearchProject","name":"Post-AGI Economics","subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/","identifier":"content:post-agi-economics-home","@type":"WebPage","name":"When intelligence gets cheap","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/mentions/","identifier":"content:one-yardstick-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"One Yardstick mentions","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":5,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/from-compute-to-an-economic-question/#article","@type":"BlogPosting","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/from-compute-to-an-economic-question/","identifier":"content:getharsh-compute-economic-question","name":"When the compute question became larger"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/a-forecast-meets-its-outcome/#article","@type":"TechArticle","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/a-forecast-meets-its-outcome/","identifier":"content:post-agi-forecast-evaluation","name":"A forecast meets its outcome"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-clock-many-frontiers/#article","@type":"TechArticle","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-clock-many-frontiers/","identifier":"content:post-agi-clock-limits","name":"The time between frontiers"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/compare-complete-execution/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/compare-complete-execution/","identifier":"content:post-agi-one-yardstick-docs","name":"Compare complete execution"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":5,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-yardstick-one-clock/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-yardstick-one-clock/","identifier":"content:post-agi-one-yardstick-one-clock","name":"One Yardstick, One Clock: why the AI timeline debate misses the point"}}],"itemListOrder":"https://schema.org/ItemListOrderDescending"},"description":"Writing that discusses or references One Yardstick across the connected publications.","text":"","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/mentions/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}],"about":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/#entity","identifier":"project:one-yardstick","@type":"CreativeWork","name":"One Yardstick","subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/","identifier":"content:post-agi-one-yardstick","@type":"WebPage","name":"One Yardstick","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/"},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/mentions/","identifier":"content:one-yardstick-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"One Yardstick mentions","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/mentions/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/privacy/","identifier":"content:post-agi-economics-privacy","@type":"WebPage","name":"Privacy notice","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"description":"Website hosting, email contact, reading preferences and external reading assistance for RTEPL's editorial publications.","text":"Privacy notice\nRawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited (RTEPL) is responsible for deciding how personal data is used on rawthoughts.in, causality.in, sleepwalker.in and post-agi-economics.org, and on their subdomains, including the documentation at docs.rawthoughts.in and docs.causality.in. RTEPL publishes and operates these websites. This notice explains the information handled when you read these websites or use their contact and reading tools.\nVisiting the website\nGitHub Pages hosts these publications. Serving a page involves connection information, including your IP address and the page requested. GitHub logs visitors’ IP addresses for security, including when a visitor is not signed in. GitHub handles that information under its privacy statement and may process it outside India.\nMintlify hosts the documentation at docs.rawthoughts.in and docs.causality.in. Serving those pages involves the same connection information, which Mintlify handles under its privacy policy and may process outside India. Mintlify's own visitor telemetry is turned off for these sites. Mintlify also provides those two sites' search, page tools and display settings, under the same privacy policy; the in-browser search, assistant tools, display settings and passage links described below belong to the other websites.\nOn the other websites, search runs in your browser against each website's own index, so what you type is not sent to a search service. Your search is kept in the search page's address, so like any address it can reach GitHub with a page request, appear in your browser history and go with the address if you share it.\nIf your browser has an assistant that supports it, each page offers that assistant two read-only tools: the text of the page you are reading and the same in-browser search. They work on what the page already shows and send nothing to us; what the assistant does with what it reads follows its provider's terms.\nContacting us\nA contact link opens a new message in your own email application. The website does not receive or store the message as a form submission. If you choose to send it, your email service and the recipient's mail service handle the correspondence. Any professional work that follows has its own correspondence and agreement.\nFor privacy questions, a grievance or a request concerning your personal data, write to info@rtepl.co.in. Include enough information to identify the request, but do not send passwords or unnecessary identity documents. We respond within 30 days and may ask for proportionate verification before disclosing or changing personal data.\nReading preferences\nThe display settings remember your colour and motion choices in your browser on this device. You can change them in Settings or clear the website's stored data through your browser. These preferences are used to display the page; they stay in your browser and are not sent to us.\nAsking about a passage\nWhen you select text and choose a reading assistant, the link opens that provider with the selected passage, nearby context, the section and article titles, the publication's name, and the article and publication addresses. The passage is limited to 4,096 characters and the nearby context to 1,024. Selecting text alone prepares the link in your browser; choosing the provider sends it there.\nThe assistant is an external service. The request is included in its link, so it can also appear in your browser history. The provider's own terms, privacy policy and your account settings govern what it does with the request and any conversation that follows. Reading the article does not require using an assistant.\nHow long information is kept\nWe keep no record of visits on our own systems; GitHub and Mintlify keep their connection logs for the periods their policies set. Correspondence you send is kept for as long as the matter it concerns needs it and as the law requires.\nSecurity and incidents\nThese websites hold no accounts and receive no form submissions. If a personal-data breach affects you, we inform you and the authorities as applicable law requires.\nYour choices\nFollowing another external link takes you to that service under its own data practices.\nYou may ask us about your data, request correction or deletion, withdraw consent or raise a concern using the contact above. Requests are handled under applicable law; information needed to comply with a legal obligation or resolve a dispute may need to be kept. Withdrawing consent does not undo processing that has already occurred lawfully.\nChanges to the website's data practices will be reflected in this notice. A new optional use that requires consent will ask for that consent rather than treating continued reading as agreement.\nLast reviewed on 22 November 2026 by RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/privacy/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/research/","identifier":"content:post-agi-economics-projects","@type":"WebPage","name":"Research","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"description":"How cheaper cognitive work changes production, scarcity, household resources and the models used to understand them.","text":"A woman stands still amid a crowd moving along a city street.\nWhat breaks when thinking gets cheap\nIf cognitive work becomes abundant, what happens to the limits around it? Begin with that question, then follow what an economic model must retain as production and the people making decisions change.\nA woman stands still amid a crowd moving along a city street.\nOne Yardstick\nJudge human and machine work against the same task. Then ask what else would have to change for repeated use to affect production and household purchasing power.\nFurther questions\nWhen the model must change\nNew decision makers and relationships can ask more of an economic model than a new estimate within it. Causality studies how descriptions can change while keeping account of the distinctions that matter.\nExplore the formal inquiry\nCapability in working life\nA capability matters economically when people can use it in recurring work. RawThoughts explores the systems around that use: goals, context, relationships and the responsibility for a result.\nExplore the systems\nQuestions about the research\nWhat does this research ask?\nPost-AGI Economics asks what changes in an economy, and in the models that describe it, when cognitive work gets cheap. The main line of inquiry follows production, scarcity and who receives the gain.\nDoes this inquiry depend on predicting one AGI date?\nNo. Capability, affordable access, reliable operation and institutional change move at different speeds. The inquiry follows particular frontiers and the ways they affect one another. A machine does not need to cross one universal threshold for its economic consequences to matter.\nWhen does the economic model need to change?\nA model that represents plans and machine time can still work when machinery becomes the limit. New decision makers, ownership arrangements or relationships may require a different account. The question is which consequence the model must preserve. The worked comparisons show when to recalculate within a model and when to retain something it previously left out.\nWhy follow ownership and income alongside productivity?\nProducing more does not determine who receives the gain. Prices, wages, ownership claims and public arrangements connect a production improvement to the resources of particular households. Those channels also affect demand and the next production decision.\nHow does One Yardstick fit the programme?\nOne Yardstick begins with a fair comparison of human and agent execution on a defined task, then asks what recurring economic use would require. The wider programme follows adoption, moving constraints and distribution across many such changes. One Yardstick’s proposed transition clock asks how much time an economy has to adapt, and the programme’s worked examples compare what follows under stated conditions.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://post-agi-economics.org/assets/content/post-agi-economics/media/opening/card-light.png","description":"A woman stands still amid a crowd moving along a city street."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://post-agi-economics.org/assets/content/post-agi-economics/media/opening/card-light.png","description":"A woman stands still amid a crowd moving along a city street."}],"url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/research/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/search/","identifier":"content:post-agi-economics-search","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Search","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":15,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/history-without-a-timetable/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/history-without-a-timetable/","identifier":"content:post-agi-history-mechanisms","name":"A catalogue still needs an audience"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/a-forecast-meets-its-outcome/#article","@type":"TechArticle","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/a-forecast-meets-its-outcome/","identifier":"content:post-agi-forecast-evaluation","name":"A forecast meets its outcome"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/about/","@type":"AboutPage","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/about/","identifier":"content:post-agi-economics-about","name":"About Post-AGI Economics"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/capital-theory/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/moving-constraints/capital-theory/","identifier":"content:post-agi-capital","name":"After the planning bottleneck"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":5,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/","identifier":"content:post-agi-economics-articles","name":"Blog"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":6,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/cheap-copies-scarce-attention/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/cheap-copies-scarce-attention/","identifier":"content:post-agi-attention","name":"Cheap copies, scarce attention"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":7,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/compare-complete-execution/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/compare-complete-execution/","identifier":"content:post-agi-one-yardstick-docs","name":"Compare complete execution"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":8,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/measuring-ai-diffusion/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/measuring-ai-diffusion/","identifier":"content:post-agi-measuring-diffusion","name":"Counting the spread of AI"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":9,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/from-abundance-to-a-changed-description/","identifier":"journey:post-agi-abundance-and-description","name":"From abundance to a changed description"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":10,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/who-gets-the-gain/from-capability-to-economic-consequence/","identifier":"journey:post-agi-economic-consequence","name":"From capability to economic consequence"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":11,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/collections/thinking-gets-cheap/models-and-measures/","identifier":"collection:post-agi-models-measures","name":"Models and measures"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":12,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/","identifier":"content:post-agi-one-yardstick","name":"One Yardstick"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":13,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/mentions/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/one-yardstick/mentions/","identifier":"content:one-yardstick-mentions","name":"One Yardstick mentions"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":14,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-yardstick-one-clock/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/one-yardstick-one-clock/","identifier":"content:post-agi-one-yardstick-one-clock","name":"One Yardstick, One Clock: why the AI timeline debate misses the point"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":15,"item":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/privacy/","@type":"WebPage","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/privacy/","identifier":"content:post-agi-economics-privacy","name":"Privacy notice"}}]},"description":"Find pages, articles and collections from Post-AGI Economics.","text":"","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/search/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/","identifier":"content:post-agi-economics-terms","@type":"WebPage","name":"Terms of use","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/"},"description":"Use and reuse of RTEPL's editorial publications and contact with the publisher.","text":"Terms of use\nRawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited (RTEPL) publishes and operates RawThoughts, Causality, Sleepwalker and Post-AGI Economics. These terms concern your use of rawthoughts.in, causality.in, sleepwalker.in and post-agi-economics.org, and of every subdomain of them, such as the documentation at docs.rawthoughts.in and docs.causality.in.\nReading and reusing material\nUnless a page states its own licence, the content of these four publications, their text, images and data, is not openly licensed. You may read it, link to its pages and quote passages from it with attribution: name the author or publication and link to the page you quote. We permit that use, and it leaves untouched any wider right you have under fair dealing (section 52 of India's Copyright Act, 1957) or fair use elsewhere. The permission covers accurate quotation only; it does not cover misquoting the material or quoting it out of context so that it says something it does not. Reproducing the material without attribution, or presenting it as your own, is not permitted.\nTraining AI models on this material is not permitted, whether it is collected from these websites, their subdomains or any other copy, and training crawlers are refused. Search engines and answer systems that link to the pages and cite them remain welcome; only training is refused. This reservation is also our opt-out from text and data mining wherever the law provides one. Each website and subdomain carries it in its robots.txt and a machine-readable reservation wherever its host lets us publish them; where a host does not, this page still applies. These terms do not withdraw permissions granted by a licence or change a third party's rights.\nFor other copying, adaptation or redistribution, follow the licence attached to the page, asset or source, or ask us or the rights holder named on the item. Attribution alone does not replace permission.\nAuthors, contributors and third parties retain the rights attributed to them. Quotations, translations, images and software do not acquire a common licence merely by appearing on this website. A software repository's licence governs that software, not all the prose or images published here.\nThe scope of the publication\nThe publications cover applied ideas, economic inquiry, research and creative work. Read a research model with its stated assumptions and limits; its description does not guarantee how a deployed system will perform. Economic arguments and scenarios offer neither personal investment, tax or financial advice nor a transaction or guaranteed forecast. Interpretations of source texts, fiction and artistic experiments should be read in that form, rather than taken as medical guidance or scientific findings.\nConsulting work, software use, support and service levels follow their own agreements. Reading these pages or sending an enquiry does not enter you into one. Software, datasets, source texts, translations and contributed works keep their own licences and rights.\nWe aim to keep the material accurate and correct errors brought to our attention. Sources, methods and circumstances can change; a dated account concerns the setting it describes. You remain responsible for assessing whether material is suitable for your circumstances and for seeking qualified advice where needed.\nResponsible use\nDo not use the website to infringe another person's rights, expose confidential information, impersonate someone, interfere with its operation or attempt unauthorised access.\nExternal websites and services have their own terms. A reference is not an assurance of their accuracy, security or continued availability.\nAvailability and responsibility\nThe publication is provided as information. We do not promise uninterrupted access or that every example will suit a particular purpose. To the extent permitted by applicable law, we make no warranty of fitness for your use and are not responsible for loss arising from reliance on the material or interruption of access. Nothing here excludes a responsibility or remedy that the law does not allow us to exclude.\nPrivacy and contact\nThe privacy notice explains the information handled by this publication’s website and reading tools. A new use requiring consent will ask for it; continued reading is not agreement to that use.\nFor a correction, permission request or concern about these terms, write to info@rtepl.co.in. Please identify the page and the issue so we can respond.\nGoverning law\nIndian law governs these terms. Subject to mandatory law and any jurisdiction that cannot lawfully be excluded, disputes concerning use of the website fall within the jurisdiction of the competent courts in Lucknow. These terms do not replace a separate contract, licence or statutory right.\n","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/terms/","publisher":{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/#organization","identifier":"organization:rtepl","@type":"Organization","name":"RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarshOnline"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/blog/oldest/","@type":"CollectionPage","author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","@type":"Person","identifier":"person:harsh","name":"Harsh Joshi","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}],"url":"https://getharsh.in/"}],"creativeWorkStatus":"published","description":"Independent essays on exchanging intelligence, comparing human and agent work, timing